(57290, 8714, 1013, 100276, 1072936800, '', 'What\'s wrong with it? All benchmarks I\'ve seen show it\'s the 5600 that lags behind in performance, not the 5800 (especially Ultra).. The 5900 is offcourse the best, but also very expensive..\n\n\n\nhttp://www6.tomshardware.com/graphi...d_guide-13.html (http://www6.tomshardware.com/graphic/20030714/vga_card_guide-13.html)\n\n\n\nEdit: http://www6.tomshardware.com/graphi...d_guide-08.html (http://www6.tomshardware.com/graphic/20030714/vga_card_guide-08.html)\n\nThe 5900 \'value\' version looks interesting (maybe)..', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(55947, 8809, 1013, 100430, 1072936800, 'The deal about gcache.gcf', 'Because of the surfacing information about having spyware that detects mp3 files and kazaa, i have on request from some people edited the original post, and it\'s sub-post.\n\n\n\n\n\nA quick reminder. A harddrive would not by itself execute any kind of code.\n\n\n\n\n\nFurthermore the appearances of MP3 is only directed at ingame MP3 music(Which Half-Life music is changed too, but not working yet). And config/menu files including MP3.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe appearances of Kazaa is only chained together with a other set of useless chars remind a lot of a name like kazaa, (kakakaka, zazazaza, zaazaazaa, azazaz, and so on. While appearences of "kazaa" occur there is no such thing  as your username in the harddrive, if there is, it\'s because you have gotten a changed copy of steam or you put it in it yourself.)\n\n\n\n\n\nAnd this has been verified by 8 people, and then verified again by me. I can\'t remember the exact numbers but i believe 2 had the official KMD, while the rest used Kazaa lite. I also installed kazaa lite.\n\n\n\n\n\nEdit: I\'m sorry the information had to go, and i\'m sorry for making all replies useless.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(55985, 8809, 1013, 103162, 1072936800, '', 'well said though it is wierd they would require 400mb download down your throat when it is mostly empty, but most likely there is a reason for this so I won\'t argue..\n\n\n\n\n\n--KallDrexx', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(55998, 8809, 1013, 103163, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by KallDrexx \n\n\nwell said though it is wierd they would require 400mb download down your throat when it is mostly empty, but most likely there is a reason for this so I won\'t argue..\n\n\n\n\n\n--KallDrexx \n\n\nIt\'s not clear to me what you mean with your post[]\n\n\nBut steam only downloads the files it needs(200MB) and puts them all into a big cache file(400MB) which has enough space left to put in other files\n\n\n\n\n\nI think the files is so big that steam won\'t run out of space while downlading a update for a game', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(55999, 8809, 1013, 100546, 1072936800, '', 'Once the video has been extracted from the cache file, does it then flag that space in the gcache so it can be re-used?\n\n\n\n\n\nI would assume it does, otherwise what happens when the cache gets full []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(56196, 8809, 1013, 103164, 1072936800, '', 'The cache file is created by steam as a blank file.  You don\'t download the 400MB.\n\n\n\n\n\nSteam puts stuff into the file as it is downloaded.\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is how steam appeared to download 1.5GB in less than an hour on my system.  It created a bunch of empty space to be filled in later.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
