(58075, 9035, 1013, 102117, 1072936800, '', 'OMG it looks like I am going to have to do my 3 page post on the human eye all over again, when they released v2 of steam they took all but essential posts down mine was kept for about 3 months so here we go again\n\n\n\n\n\nI did a thesis for Uni on they human eye explaining the fps of games and they way the yeye interprets them\n\n\n\n\n\nThe human eye sees at over 300fps\n\n\n\n\n\nUK TV is broadcast at 25.2fps\n\n\nUS TV (non HDTV) is broadcast at 35fps\n\n\nUS TV (HDTV) is broadcast at 60fps\n\n\n\n\n\nThe reason that alot of people belive the human eye only sees at 25-30fps is because the human brain actually fills in alot of animation for itself, the actually display of a monitor doesnt even get close to 100fps, but becasue of the refresh rate, graphics cards actually make the refresh rate react at something like 100-150fps, when in actuall fact the refresh rate of the eye and the refresh rate of the monitor are not in sync at all\n\n\n\n\n\nThe human eye refreshes at something like 25fps that is the amount of times the eye blinks when in actuall fact the eye sees at 100fps that is why sometimes you see the black when you blink.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe discovery channel quote is of the human blink not of the human refresh.\n\n\n\n\n\nIf you want to test how fast your eye can refresh.\n\n\n\n\n\nGet a friend to hold a pen about 5-10cm from your face them make them move it side-to-side very fast, (without hitting you) if you can see the pen moving and not just a blur then your eye is refreshing at about 70fps, if you only see a blur you are seeing at 35fps, the reason for hte difference is that looking at a computer monitor actually damages teh human eye and our brains try to compensate by going at 100fps so our vision gets jerky.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe reason you can tell the difference at under 30fps compared to about is because when it goes below 50fps (actually) the human brain has too much to calculate and misses things, which is why it seems jerky on screen. if its above 70fps then they human brain doesnt have to think that much, so it doesnt seem jerky, if it goes above 100fps then the human brain almost switches off the process to compensate.\n\n\n\n\n\nNow then if anyone did actually read that, i think that has answered any questions.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(58080, 9035, 1013, 100114, 1072936800, '', 'I read it all... I READ IT ALL w00t for me. And yes, it has really answered the question... but wait, I can\'t judge that, only the one who asked can tell.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
