(97101, 14270, 1013, 101008, 1072936800, 'Why don\'t you guys understand firewalls?', 'It may be a simple point, but saying "open" to a network administrator will just produce sniggers.\n\n\n\n\n\nDoes "open" mean open to *incoming* traffic, or *outgoing* traffic?\n\n\n\n\n\nIt\'s not as contentious to open a port for outgoing traffic (TCP or UDP) since the user at least initiated the connection, so may have some idea of what they\'re doing. (Guffaws of laughter)\n\n\n\n\n\nHowever, opening an incoming port to TCP traffic which will connect to a potentially buggy and very likely insecure piece of software is like posting a big sign "Hack Me Here". It\'s an invitation to all the nasty people to try and break the software and take over the PC.\n\n\n\n\n\nMost firewalls will allow outgoing traffic by default, but block incoming connections by default.\n\n\n\n\n\nIt may be that Steam requires inbound ports to be open, in which case, it\'s extremely poor design and the product will be short-lived. I assume that the designers are aware of this (even if they aren\'t aware that a 1GB download will block their outbound pipepes), so it may be the case that a little more clarity is required.\n\n\n\n\n\nPlease can you say what you mean, and get them to update the FAQ if necessary?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(97194, 14270, 1013, 100999, 1072936800, '', 'Well seeing as I\'m not on the steam tech team (god help them if I was) I really can\'t get them to change what\'s on the FAQ, However as far as "opening" ports goes, I was under the assumption that simply allows traffic to pass through like they described. I\'m sorry if I\'m vague, networking is my second forte as opposed to tech specs and whatnot', '127.0.0.1', 1),
