(144926, 22552, 1013, 102084, 1072936800, 'What the Source leak means to Valve', 'I\'ve been trawling through the code for a few hours and found a few interesting bits. I can see various portions of code thats in there being lifted directly and used in a game thats halfway through development.\n\n\n\nIt also exposes a fair few holes that can easily be exploited in online play if put into the wrong hands - which it no doubt already is. The most worrying thing about all this is its likely to delay HL2 for even longer because they\'ve got to patch these holes in some way. The worst thing that happened to the current run of Half-Life games was the release of the SDK, armed with this kind of indepth knowledge of the engine a skilled coder can do pretty much what they please. \n\n\n\nTheres one other point, I expect Valve were expecting to make quite a financial gain on licensing the engine out for third party titles, no doubt its a well sort after engine and most games developers would give their eye teeth for what is in here. \n\n\n\nPoor old valve have been kicked in the face here and I feel really sorry for them, a worse thing couldn\'t have happened at this point. I hope that any game devs out there are gentlemanly enough to disregard what they can learn from this and give Valve the respect they\'re due.\n\n\n\nMy only hope is that its an older build, most of the dates in there are from 2000 - 2002, I haven\'t noticed 2003 yet... Yet.\n\n__________________\n\nIf you aren\'t part of the cure then you\'re part of the problem.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
