(151572, 23502, 1005, 102167, 1072936800, '', 'I LOVE BOTS, oh why oh why dont they put back in bots. when ever my self esteem was down id just give bots knives and kill them. they improved my aim. they made me feel like i had someone when I was lonely. wed had drinks together, party together, i lost my virginity to a POD bot. oh why oh why dont they bring back my bots, i love them more then my own wife.\n\n\nwaita.... i dont have a wife. anywayz', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(153562, 23991, 1013, 102168, 1072936800, 'will Valve publically address security concerns from hl2 code leak?', 'With the recent hl2 code leak, I\'m concerned about HL2 and what steps valve will take to ensure proper security of the gaming experience and of everyone\'s future purchase.\n\n\n\n\n\nIn particular, I\'m hoping that Valve will make a public statement that will satisfy the community that proper measures have been taken.\n\n\n\n\n\nWhat I\'m most concerned about:\n\n\n\n\n\n- That the cd-key is never transmitted to a privately run game server in a form that can be reversed to get the original cd-key--what I\'m thinking about here is, now that the code is out, it would be simple for someone to break their algorithm and get the key.  What would be better is either to transmit part of the key that can be verified without the rest, or to transmit a hash.  If the full key is ever sent directly to the game server, cheat and hacker groups will just open key-collector servers so they can gather thousands of cd-key\'s and then proceed to cheat and do other things with impunity.\n\n\n\n\n\n- That Steam hasn\'t been compromised by this leak, and thus will be able to authenticate hl2 clients to ensure that people are not using customized cheat versions, and also that the steam system hasn\'t had enough code exposed to enable a hacker to hijack it to install malware on thousands of peoples\' PC\'s.\n\n\n\n\n\n- That the servers which handle billing for Steam are not directly connected to the Valve dev. network in a way in which "trusted" PC\'s that could potentially be compromised in the future can access them with lower security restrictions.  I really don\'t want to buy myg0t and friends a brand new video card with my credit card info.\n\n\n\n\n\nIf you\'re concerned about issues like this, and want Valve to address these issues publically or at least give us some reasonable assurance, please add your comments.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(153567, 23991, 1013, 102169, 1072936800, '', 'Well put, I also think Valve need to be open about the situation. \n\n\n\n\n\nIf there are things that are compromised beyond repair, don\'t be quiet about hoping the hackers won\'t figure it out... \n\n\nJust let the community know and we\'d be happy to wait for the game while you recode.\n\n__________________\n\n*Help! the paranoids are stalkling me!*', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(153571, 23991, 1013, 102170, 1072936800, '', 'Just some rumors:\n\n\n\n\n\nNow get this, several team members from Valve broke the lockdown and have made a few comments (no identification, they wished to remain anonymous, but they are confirmed to be legit Valve employees). Most distressing is this... they said that Valve is looking at a minimum of SIX lawsuits, possibily more depending on the total damage. And that there have also been talk WITHIN Valve of scrapping the entire game. They also claimed that an estimated 1.32GB of data was transferred off Valve\'s computers, which matches the 1.32GB beta reported to be stolen.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe latest development is this... starting yesterday several simple, custom-made maps were released that allowed people to walk around in a small room using the HL2 engine. Today, the people have dug up and activated the MULTIPLAYER code... and IT WORKS. \n\n\n\n\n\nThe latest rumour is this... the full beta will be released on the internet at midnight tonight, but there has been no confirmation of this. \n\n\n\n\n\nAccording to a number of inside-sources, things may get a lot worst, and the worst-case scenario is the cancellation of Half-Life 2 (a probability) and the shutting down of Valve (less likely but still possible at this point due to the lawsuits).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSo yeah i would also want to hear what valve has to say, instead of all of these probably fake rumors.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(153575, 23991, 1013, 102171, 1072936800, 'will Valve publically address security concerns from hl2 code leak?', 'quote:Originally posted by Barbarian \n\n\nWith the recent hl2 code leak, I\'m concerned about HL2 and what steps valve will take to ensure proper security of the gaming experience and of everyone\'s future purchase.\n\n\n\n\n\nIn particular, I\'m hoping that Valve will make a public statement that will satisfy the community that proper measures have been taken.\n\n\n\n\n\nWhat I\'m most concerned about :\n\n\n\n\n\n- That the cd-key is never transmitted to a privately run game server in a form that can be reversed to get the original cd-key--what I\'m thinking about here is, now that the code is out, it would be simple for someone to break their algorithm and get the key.  What would be better is either to transmit part of the key that can be verified without the rest, or to transmit a hash.  If the full key is ever sent directly to the game server, cheat and hacker groups will just open key-collector servers so they can gather thousands of cd-key\'s and then proceed to cheat and do other things with impunity.\n\n\n\n\n\n- That Steam hasn\'t been compromised by this leak, and thus will be able to authenticate hl2 clients to ensure that people are not using customized cheat versions, and also that the steam system hasn\'t had enough code exposed to enable a hacker to hijack it to install malware on thousands of peoples\' PC\'s.\n\n\n\n\n\n- That the servers which handle billing for Steam are not directly connected to the Valve dev. network in a way in which "trusted" PC\'s that could potentially be compromised in the future can access them with lower security restrictions.  I really don\'t want to buy myg0t and friends a brand new video card with my credit card info.\n\n\n\n\n\nIf you\'re concerned about issues like this, and want Valve to address these issues publically or at least give us some reasonable assurance, please add your comments.  \n\n\n\n\n\nKey is transmitted as a hash.\n\n\n\n\n\nNext point its called VAC source was included\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLast one I can\'t comment on I ain\'t at valve.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(153770, 23991, 1013, 102020, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by d-struct \n\n\nJust some rumors:\n\n\n\n\n\nNow get this, several team members from Valve broke the lockdown and have made a few comments (no identification, they wished to remain anonymous, but they are confirmed to be legit Valve employees). Most distressing is this... they said that Valve is looking at a minimum of SIX lawsuits, possibily more depending on the total damage. And that there have also been talk WITHIN Valve of scrapping the entire game. They also claimed that an estimated 1.32GB of data was transferred off Valve\'s computers, which matches the 1.32GB beta reported to be stolen.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe latest development is this... starting yesterday several simple, custom-made maps were released that allowed people to walk around in a small room using the HL2 engine. Today, the people have dug up and activated the MULTIPLAYER code... and IT WORKS. \n\n\n\n\n\nThe latest rumour is this... the full beta will be released on the internet at midnight tonight, but there has been no confirmation of this. \n\n\n\n\n\nAccording to a number of inside-sources, things may get a lot worst, and the worst-case scenario is the cancellation of Half-Life 2 (a probability) and the shutting down of Valve (less likely but still possible at this point due to the lawsuits).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSo yeah i would also want to hear what valve has to say, instead of all of these probably fake rumors.  \n\n\n\n\n\nu got a link to these rumors? sound like good reading...\n\n__________________\n\nCoca-Cola and Armageden, we like it, like it, yes we do.\n\n\n\n\n\nCoca-Cola and Armageden, it\'s the real thing now, come and get it!!!\n\n\n\n\n\n(12 ounce Epilogue >>> CLUTCH)', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(154018, 23991, 1013, 100145, 1072936800, '', 'Yup, I would really like to see Valve make some kind of official announcement about the source code theft, what it means for HL2/Steam and how they are addressing the potential future security threat.\n\n\n\n\n\nGiven the long history of lack of PR from Valve, I\'m not holding my breath though...\n\n\n\n\n\n[]\n\n__________________\n\n\'It\'s a casual, casual, easy thing...\'', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(154053, 23991, 1013, 102172, 1072936800, '', 'Gabe Newell did release something asking for help... if VALVe are going to make a statement like that then they\'re worried.\n\n__________________\n\nFish', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(154084, 23991, 1013, 100145, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by thefish_uk \n\n\nGabe Newell did release something asking for help... if VALVe are going to make a statement like that then they\'re worried. \n\n\nYou mean the post to HL2.net forums? AFAIK, that\'s the only official announcement there is, which is a bit pathetic, given the amount of media/public interest.\n\n\n\n\n\nWe need more!\n\n__________________\n\n\'It\'s a casual, casual, easy thing...\'', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(154353, 23991, 1013, 100110, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by d-struct \n\n\nJust some rumors:\n\n\n\n\n\nNow get this, several team members from Valve broke the lockdown and have made a few comments (no identification, they wished to remain anonymous, but they are confirmed to be legit Valve employees). Most distressing is this... they said that Valve is looking at a minimum of SIX lawsuits, possibily more depending on the total damage. And that there have also been talk WITHIN Valve of scrapping the entire game. They also claimed that an estimated 1.32GB of data was transferred off Valve\'s computers, which matches the 1.32GB beta reported to be stolen.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe latest development is this... starting yesterday several simple, custom-made maps were released that allowed people to walk around in a small room using the HL2 engine. Today, the people have dug up and activated the MULTIPLAYER code... and IT WORKS. \n\n\n\n\n\nThe latest rumour is this... the full beta will be released on the internet at midnight tonight, but there has been no confirmation of this. \n\n\n\n\n\nAccording to a number of inside-sources, things may get a lot worst, and the worst-case scenario is the cancellation of Half-Life 2 (a probability) and the shutting down of Valve (less likely but still possible at this point due to the lawsuits).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSo yeah i would also want to hear what valve has to say, instead of all of these probably fake rumors.  \n\n\n\n\n\ngabe said in a new email to hl2.net that the game Will come out.\n\n\n\n\n\nso don\'t worry about it being cancelled.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(154365, 23991, 1013, 100059, 1072936800, 'will Valve publically address security concerns from hl2 code leak?', 'quote:Originally posted by Barbarian \n\n\n- That the cd-key is never transmitted to a privately run game server in a form that can be reversed to get the original cd-key--what I\'m thinking about here is, now that the code is out, it would be simple for someone to break their algorithm and get the key.  What would be better is either to transmit part of the key that can be verified without the rest, or to transmit a hash.  If the full key is ever sent directly to the game server, cheat and hacker groups will just open key-collector servers so they can gather thousands of cd-key\'s and then proceed to cheat and do other things with impunity.\n\n\n\n\n\nI wouldn\'t worry, they will just change the source code\'s formula on how the CD-Key is generated. Even further the CD-Key when you register it will be locked into your steamID.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nquote:\n\n\n- That Steam hasn\'t been compromised by this leak, and thus will be able to authenticate hl2 clients to ensure that people are not using customized cheat versions, and also that the steam system hasn\'t had enough code exposed to enable a hacker to hijack it to install malware on thousands of peoples\' PC\'s.\n\n\n\n\n\nIf it was, though it doens\'t appear to be, then a simple few updates and a checksum will fix it. Another good feature about steam.\n\n\n\n\n\nquote:\n\n\n- That the servers which handle billing for Steam are not directly connected to the Valve dev. network in a way in which "trusted" PC\'s that could potentially be compromised in the future can access them with lower security restrictions.  I really don\'t want to buy myg0t and friends a brand new video card with my credit card info.\n\n\n\n\n\nI wouldn\'t worry about this either. They will most likely have a third party handle the billing not to mention even if someone does get your credit card number, and it can happen not just with valve but anyone could hack into somewhere else you did business, you should have a 0% liability clause for Internet purchases. If you don\'t then its time to cancel that card.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
