(167881, 26299, 1013, 102243, 1072936800, '', 'Actually the only reason you where probably banned was because you linked them to steampowered downloads. Steam doesnt want to have to provide space for people to download from them. It cost them money, and they aint cool with that. That\'s what steam is really, a way to save money by eliminating middle men (retailers; EB, CompUSA, etc..) that will be selling half life2, whenever valve gets around to finishing the game. See the strategy here is; why sell the game to a retailer at  wholesale price when we can simply sell it directly to the customer at full retail price through steam and make that much more money. I guess these are the tactics that valve/steam has to resort to considering they havent put out anything significant in the gaming world for what, 4-5 years? I mean go look at their website, http://www.valvesoftware.com/projects.htm (/valvesoftware/projects.htm)  they are still bragging about all the awards HalfLife has won since 1998. \n\n\n\nThe fact is this, and I hope others agree. I will not be buying HalfLife2 with my credit card though steam, considering that they can\'t keep their most valuable asset secure, the HL2 source code. So they tell us that their network is NOT secure, then they ask us for our credit card info.  Ummmmm no, I dont think so Steam. That is, unless this is all a clever conspiracy they whipped up. Meaning that they just "kinda let the code slip out there" so that they\'d have an excuse to delay the project. The project that is nowhere near finished, trust me. Because you know what? Steam is just not quite as dilligent as we would like them to be.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
