(243671, 38988, 1005, 102444, 1072936800, 'Open Letter to Valve concerning netcode', 'To whomever it may concern,\n\n\n\nHaving been an avid counter-strike player for approximately 3 years\n\nnow, I am baffled by the change that has been distributed to all Steam\n\nenabled users this past day. It is completely undocumented, as is the\n\nnew c4timer-icon by the way, and rather confusing.\n\n\n\nIt is a common opinion that "all ms is bad ms", allthough I myself am\n\nused to having it due to an ISDN connection, alot of people are rather\n\nfrustrated having gone from what they call "0ms" to approximately 50.\n\nHaving not experienced any noticeable change in the amount myself I\n\nproceeded to toy around with my netcode settings, and the results were\n\nmildly put shocking and confusing.\n\n\n\nIn all past versions I can remember cl_updaterate has been set as high\n\nas possible. This rendered the best possible connection and a feeling\n\nof "0 ms" due to the exclusion of the figure in the netgraph once the\n\nvalue dropped beneath a certain value. I noticed now that the effect of\n\nconfiguring cl_updaterate is the exact opposite of the before-mentioned\n\nversions . An updaterate of 100 renders a high ms, and an updaterate of\n\n10 renders a showing of so-called "0 ms". My question is therefore if\n\nyou could provide me with:\n\n\n\n1) An explanation of what "ms" actually is, once and for all.\n\n2) An explanation of how cl_updaterate affects the value, and what\n\nchanges have been made to the setting since the previous version.\n\n\n\nAny additional information regarding changes in the netcode, and also\n\nany good explanations of how it all works that you happen to have\n\nlying around would be greatly appreciated.\n\n\n\nBest regards and thanks for a good game,\n\nDaniel Blekkan', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244178, 38988, 1005, 102444, 1072936800, '', '*bump*\n\n\n\nC\'mon guys, help me out here [] But keep it mature please, we want Valve to respond, not ignore us due to excessive whining or use of profanity.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244222, 38988, 1005, 100014, 1072936800, '', 'I agree with all the infomation the above person has been saying i agree that we all need to know what exatcly cl_cmdrate and cl_updaterate do i use to get 0 ms with 100 100 but now i get 40-50 this feels weird and a lil slower for some reason so i also ask what is it these 2 commands do and next time plz explain yourself better so this community can get the best out of Counter-Strike Gaming....\n\n\n\nDark-Force\n\n[k]illerz < Counter-Strike New Zealand Clan >', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244249, 38988, 1005, 102445, 1072936800, '', '"cl_updaterate/cl_cmdrate \n\nWhy the default values are relatively low is because the game is designed to work for players with every type of connection and give them a smooth game play over the Internet. Another reason is that HLDS should work on most computers and not cause an immense load on the CPU. \n\n\n\nAs explained in my previous net code column the cl_updaterate and cl_cmdrate controls the server-client and client-server traffic. The higher values, the more traffic. However using low values won\'t create an inaccurate picture of the gaming world (that will only occur if the client starts to drop a lot of packets) because there doesn\'t need to be a one-to-one correspondence with the way the Half-Life engine works. \n\n\n\nThe bad thing about using too high cl_updaterate and cl_cmdrates is that: \n\n\n\nYour client bandwidth doesn\'t handle all the traffic and you receive choke (as explained above). \n\nThe server you are playing on has to work under a lot higher pressure since much smaller and more movements are sent to the server for calculation which consumes CPU power and can cause server-lag. \n\nThe important factor here is to set values depending on personal choice and what your bandwidth and computer actually can handle. On LAN and in tournaments, these values should be set high for best consistency."\n\n\n\nTaken from # http://www.schroet.com/features.php...2&insider=0 (http://www.schroet.com/features.php?id=34&page=2&insider=0) # Author: bds \n\n\n\nIf you look at that page, bds explains everything about the netcode. You need to register for Shroet to read the rest, makes interesting reading', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244253, 38988, 1005, 102445, 1072936800, '', 'Another point: if your getting loss on a server dont bother playing at all, its sommit that isnt worth playing with as it is pure packet loss, blah blah', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244256, 38988, 1005, 102444, 1072936800, '', 'Thank you for replying, however I am familiar with bds and his articles concerning the netcode. All of these articles however were written before the latest update of CS, and also none of them actually tell us what the value of "ms" portrays.\n\n\n\nAlthough I have the greatest confidence in bds and his knowledge of the game, I prefer information directly from the source over second-hand information. Also, I have yet to see an explanation of the change in cl_updaterate from bds.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244259, 38988, 1005, 102446, 1072936800, '', 'Mooo \n\n\n\nI knew You were goin to say that Daniel []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244278, 38988, 1005, 102445, 1072936800, '', 'Yer, but it is an explaination of the 1.6 code apparently http://www.quake.se/?p=news&n=846 (http://www.quake.se/?p=news&n=846) as thats where my source came for and it clearly states "BDS EXPLAINS NETCODE IN CS 1.6"\n\n\n\nNvd. gl waiting from a explaination here, not many admins ( if any ) have technical knowledge of the HL net code.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244293, 38988, 1005, 102444, 1072936800, '', 'Once again, the change I am talking about appeared with the latest update of CS. In other words the one that was distributed the 26 and 27 November.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244302, 38988, 1005, 102446, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by Mac182 \n\nYer, but it is an explaination of the 1.6 code apparently http://www.quake.se/?p=news&n=846 (http://www.quake.se/?p=news&n=846) as thats where my source came for and it clearly states "BDS EXPLAINS NETCODE IN CS 1.6"\n\n\n\nNvd. gl waiting from a explaination here, not many admins ( if any ) have technical knowledge of the HL net code.  \n\n\n\nOMG\n\n\n\nEither read his posts, or STFU!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244413, 38988, 1005, 102445, 1072936800, '', 'Opacus least i "tired" to post sommit constructive, instead of n00bie flammerage by the likes of you, you have posted twice on this and contributed jack, so it is u that should stf*u tbh. Sad little flammers like you need to get a life tbh, instead of trying to just increase his/her amount of posts.\n\n\n\nIf you pld cs for 3 yrs you should understand things like cmdrate updaterate and the old pushlatency. Valve have stated they have NOT changed the netcode at all for the 1.6 steam transition or any updates since.\n\n\n\nTo answer you 2 questions:\n\n\n\n1)"ms" is the time it takes for you to send a packet to the server and recieve a packet in return. a ping of 50 ms means it will take 50 milli seconds to send and recieve a packet, however, overheads such as loss and choke can ditort this and cause packet loss. Increasing cl_cmdrate and cl_updaterate can increase the rate of the packets sent and recived, hence the increase in in/out rates on your netgraph, however, can increase your ping from say 35->50 as the packets are more frequent. It is more preferable to have a higher ping but more packet communication with the server in my experience, the bullet reg is generally better, so not all ms is bad ms.\n\n\n\n2)cl_updaterate outcome has not changed since the previous versions, however, value seemed to have reversed the effects on the ms value reported by the netgrapah. Low updatrates now appear to Lower ping vice versa. ppl are reporting sluggishness, however the netcode has not changed only the crappy output on ppls netgraph, so ppl with 101/101 may say their ping is higher clientside, where infact it isnt.\n\n\n\nAll you should do is tinker with these values and get the flatest netgraph possible that is the best way to get the best/fastest connection possible imho, personally i have v/sync on, 60 HZ refresh, 60/60/20000.\n\n\n\nLast thing if you can find the servers  sv_maxupdaterate as there isnt much point whacking it to 101 when its 70, you will probably get choke or sommit for over requesting packets.\n\n\n\nLet me know if this helps or if anything i said is baffling or wrong blah blah.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244453, 38988, 1005, 102444, 1072936800, '', 'Well, and I am really trying to keep my temper here, could you please explain the difference between the value of ms in our "crappy netgraphs" as you so eloquently put it and the ping in the scoreboard? \n\n\n\nAnd for the record, it is in no way a more accurate latency measure. I play on an ISDN connection every day, and by fiddling with cl_cmd and update- rate I can lower my "ms" to 10 and even under, granted suffering from choke but still it is under 10. My ping, shown on the scoreboard, however will at this amount of data have risen to at least 80. Also I know that even with absolutely no dataflow whatsoever a ping of 10ms is absolutely impossible considering my connection and my ISP\'s route to the server.\n\n\n\nTherefore I would like to point out that ms is in fact an abbreviation of milliseconds, and that it could be measuring anything for all we know. This point is precisely why I have asked Valve for an explanation and answer to my question.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244459, 38988, 1005, 102447, 1072936800, '', 'Well I cant really add any constructive thinking to this post. \n\n\n\nBut I would like to add my story of what happened after the latest update 26/27-11-03.\n\n\n\nAlmost everybody I know, and me have experienced a ms of about 20 konstantly, and only after the new update.\n\n\n\nAnd no matter what I do I can\'t get rid of it. rate cl_updaterate, cmdrate and so on doesn\'t change anything.\n\n\n\nI got a 512/512 ADSL connection and it have never been a problem before. \n\n\n\nSo it would be nice to know what they changed, in the last update apart from showing the VAC client side, player shadows(can they be disabled). weapons and so on. \n\n\n\nBecause I see many other things happening. Like keys no longer working, funny text appearing when trying to use buyscript.\n\n\n\nSo  all in all nice to know things :-)\n\n\n\nThanks\n\nMalum', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244496, 38988, 1005, 102445, 1072936800, '', 'Im sorry, but you aren\'t gonna get a member of valves team reply to a post on a public message board policed by steam. Think about it :/ and dont go loosing your cool i am trying to help you clarify things.\n\n\n\nScoreboard = actual ping to the server. 80ms in the case you described.\n\n\n\nNetgraph = is the ping your client expects given the amount of data you send and the frequency of it. It will lie as the calculations made dont take into consideration server location / net traffice etc, it is only calculations made by your pc alone given the data it sends.\n\n\n\nYou rightly said you can lower the value appearing on the netgraph at the expense of getting a higher ping on the scoreboard. You get a higher ping on the scorebaord because you are sending more packets and recieving more (more data transfer), and your home pc will think you will have a lower ping because it is sending lots more packets, however, the increased frequencies of packet sending will give a better bullet reg and smoothness to the game.\n\n\n\nLowering the rate values to say 10/10 will give you a nice low ping on the scoreboard, and a horrible "netgrpah" ping however you are sending nothing in the way of packets. You get a lower ping to the server cause its like playing tennis with 1 ball.\n\n\n\nI have already defined \'ms\' the time it takes to send and recieve a packet from the server in milliseconds.\n\n\n\nYour \'ms\' is just really the speed your packets come back to you and tinkering the cmd and update can send more of these packets, sending more takes longer for em to come back unless you have the bandwidth', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(244504, 38988, 1005, 102444, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by Mac182 \n\nLowering the rate values to say 10/10 will give you a nice low ping on the scoreboard, and a horrible "netgrpah" ping however you are sending nothing in the way of packets.  \n\n\n\nThis is my point exactly. That is the OLD behaviour of cl_updaterate. However now, it behaves the exact opposite. Also, you are describing scoreboard ping as ACTUAL latency and netgraph ms as EXPECTED latency? That doesn\'t make even a bit of sense to me, what would be the point of the netgraph value of ms in that scenario?\n\n\n\nIt has always been my belief that scoreboard ping is the latency reported by hl and netgraph ms is the frequency of updates received measured in milliseconds. This because of the direct relationship between updaterate and ms.\n\n\n\nIf we set our client to update 100 times a second(updaterate), we will be receiving updates more frequently than when requestion 40 updates a second. With the new flipped behaviour of cl_updaterate I am not so sure any longer, and request an explanation, with some form of reference, preferably from a staff-member from Valve.\n\n\n\nAs for your statements, I appreciate the effort, but like I\'ve said before, I would like an explanation of the CHANGE made, and also a clear definition from someone who definately knows as to what ms really is.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(246009, 39421, 1005, 102451, 1072936800, 'I know why servers restart now !!!!', 'I have just found out why my server restarts every so often with\n\nno errors nothing\n\n\n\nIt is dodgy CLIENTS !!!!\n\n\n\nI have found a regular on my server that as soon as he attempts to connect he brings down the server with out fail\n\nSo I thought this was a problem with my install etc but NO !!\n\nHe can restart any 9 servers out of 10 (linux) he likes just but trying to connect to them !!!\n\n(windows is ok he can connect)\n\nOn servers he doesnt restart he gets\n\nerror verifying steam user id ticket - server was unable to contact the authentication server\n\n\n\nThere must be so many dodgy clients doing this?\n\nI have always thought it could be but couldnt prove it\n\n\n\nAny idea\'s Steam People ???? \n\n\n\nAny thing at all ???', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(246140, 39421, 1005, 102452, 1072936800, '', 'Perhaps they\'ll do what they normally do ie say nothing about it at all - don\'t admit there\'s a prob bury head in sand then sneakily fix it.\n\n\n\nSurely not tho?\n\n\n\nBy the time all the bugs are ironed out of Steam people wont be interested anymore - shame really as Counter-Strike WAS nice and stable.\n\n\n\nBet Sierra are having a good giggle.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
