(272668, 43297, 1026, 102547, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by InterEver \n\nhello\n\n\n\nDelete the repertory "steam" witch is hidden in "~" , in the user main repertory\n\n\n\nnormally it have to run correctly after it\n\n\n\n\n\n++  \n\n\n\nI\'m glad to see that it worked for you. However, I\'m having trouble deciphering what this means. Could someone please translate it for me? Thanks!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273203, 43297, 1026, 102548, 1072936800, '', 'go to the root-dir.\n\n\n\n(that meant InterEver with "~").\n\n\n\ndelete the ".steam" directory and try the install again.\n\n\n\n(you can delete all directorys and files that are in the ".steam" - dir with rm -r .steam).\n\n\n\n\n\n... or use this command *g*:\n\n\n\n# rm -r /root/.steam\n\n\n\nthanks a lot, i had the same problem. this solution works for me.\n\n\n\nbye,\n\nMido', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(275760, 43297, 1026, 102550, 1072936800, '', 'I had the same issue,for weeks no one could tell me a hint of what to do,I even emailed steam but received no replay. This issue should be posted as STICKY\n\n\n\nand thanks for the fix ! works great.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(272997, 43450, 1013, 103411, 1072936800, 'A Really Cool Suggestion (Cd keys)', 'well i don\'t really trust my local HL/Counter-Strike\'s supplier/retailer or whatever they are....it gets on my nerves too everytime tis cd keys got hacked and we have no one to blame...why don\'t we just purchase directly the cd key from STEAM and registered it online wid our username/nickname, at least now, if our cd keys got prob we can blame it on STEAM....', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273023, 43450, 1013, 103412, 1072936800, '', 'Ah yes, one more thing to blame on STEAM?\n\n\n\nBetter idea, STEAM uses different CD key system and is generated randomly, I mean ENTIRELY random. No pattern or anything. This is done with ASE, but since it doesnt boot who is using the same CD key, it doesnt work that well.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273055, 43450, 1013, 101914, 1072936800, '', 'Actually, I believe the plan is to sell cd keys through Steam.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273257, 43450, 1013, 100513, 1072936800, '', 'Not CD-KEYS.\n\n\n\n\n\nYou buy the game and that game is bound to your account. That\'ll be in the future.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(275122, 43450, 1013, 103411, 1072936800, '', 'It looks like everyone agrees wid me.... spread tis idea and see if STEAM will let me have a FREE CD KEY bcoz im helping them to gain more MONEY...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(275493, 43450, 1013, 101190, 1072936800, '', 'I don\'t think there is such a thing as entirely random (when dealing with computers, anyway).  Everything is pseudo random, or uses some kind of value to generate the result (such as the exact time in hours, minutes, seconds and milli/microseconds).\n\n\n\nI\'m not 100% sure on this, feel free to correct me if anyone knows better.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(275944, 43450, 1013, 103413, 1072936800, '', 'You\'re right - however, a pseudo-random number seeded from the microseconds value of the PC clock is close enough to true random-ness to make no difference in practical terms.\n\n\n\nWhat the OP was getting at is that almost all software validates CD keys using a simple mathematical algorithm of some sort. For example, Microsoft Publisher 97 used the laughably simply system of xxxx-yyyy-zzzz, where xxxx must be divisible by 7, zzzz must be divisible by 9, and yyyy can be any value. Reversing this process is how keygens work.\n\n\n\nIf Steam issued totally random keys, and after payment gave you X days to enter your key and register it with an account, then keygenning would be nigh-on impossible because of the sheer quantity of 13 digit numbers out there.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(276144, 43450, 1013, 100349, 1072936800, '', 'As far as I know, only a tiny number of people actually have had real problems with keys off a CD.  The vast majority of ppl with key problems who we check out are just using keygen\'ed keys but say they bought it.   It\'s pretty clear though that they are using "fake" keys.  Too bad they don\'t know how obviously foolish they look sending them in and claiming they are real :^)\n\n\n\nSummary:  trust the CD key you buy at a store.  It\'s secure.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(276184, 43450, 1013, 103414, 1072936800, 'Problem with CD keys', 'My cd key im using on one screenname..\n\nI know you can only use one cd key but shouldnt it be more like per ip address?\n\n\n\nMy screenname is messed up lately and i cant figure out how to delete it. \n\nIt wont let me type in a new name w/a new cd key on my comp because my messed up steam is using it.\n\n\n\nNow all I can do is stare at my gameless steam window....or play the original where i cant get any good servers\n\n\n\nI am 36% addicted to Counterstrike. What about you? (http://seanism.com/addiction/cs.php)', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(278269, 43450, 1013, 103411, 1072936800, 'where\'s my free cd key??', 'ok....i think maybe ppl from US should have more advantages in terms of purchasing Original HL/Counter-Strike wid new Cd Keys tat can work (where\'s the manufacturer??)....If I have to wait for the original shipping to my place,,,,there\'s alot of conspiracy to hack during the way....my country is the top ten in the world infamous for its pirates... i don\'t feel proud...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(520233, 43450, 1013, 100121, 1072936800, 'how a cd key process works', 'I am a computer programmer and here is the simplest explanation:\n\nsay you have a key of 25 digits. what you do is:\n\nuse a randomize command on c++ and what it does is it circles between 0 through 9 and a through z as soon as that number/letter is determined the program stores it in a place called array (differentiating between numbers and characters)\n\nfinally because the operation has to be done 25 times we use a loop which let;s the program run and stops as soon as all 25 spaces are filled\n\nthen it sends that 25 digit key to the system and stores it there\n\nthere are no patterns whatsoever. the way randomize function is designed.. it is meant to randomize whatever on purpose.\n\n\n\nhere is a simple example (in pseudocode)\n\nfor (int key; start; end){\n\nkey_now=random_chosen;\n\nkey holder(of current step 1-25) =key_now;\n\n}\n\n}\n\nthis is a silly example but this is what the program does. it takes 1 minute to write it. it takes 1 minute to write sendtosystem function and finally the rest of the time is done printing it out, sticking into game packages etc', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(520390, 43450, 1013, 100566, 1072936800, '', 'Woot for yarik83! Sounds like an excellent idea.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(520477, 43450, 1013, 103414, 1072936800, '', 'WOOT TO THAT!!!!\n\n\n\nBut one question, if people can randomize that, couldnt they just keep choosing random numbers rapidly', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(520558, 43450, 1013, 100250, 1072936800, '', 'Depends how fast you can crunch and test new keys, but if there\'s 36 possbilities per digit of the key (letters + numbers) and 25 digits, that means there are 36^25 possible keys.\n\n\n\n808281277464764060643139600456536293376 possible keys in total.\n\n\n\nThe odds are not pleasant for finding a working key, to put it bluntly.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(520652, 43450, 1013, 103414, 1072936800, '', 'HOLY....good point, its like playing the lottery', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273695, 43535, 1013, 102551, 1072936800, 'Help!!! Steam update, wont update! "access denied"', 'I woke up this morning ready to play some Cs but this damn this is ****in up again. I started to load the program, it said it was updating. OK... no big deal. Then it reaches to 26% and steam just dissappears and it closes. Then a little error window pops up saying \n\n\n\n"Steam.exe(Main Exception) Error: delete of steam.exe failed, Win32 Error 5"Access Denied"\n\n\n\nDoes anyone know what the hell this is? im getting pissed off. So if anyone can help it would be greatly appreciated.\n\n\n\nmark', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273699, 43535, 1013, 101324, 1072936800, '', 'i just read this , and i shut down my steam , and it did update , and it did start up .\n\n\n\n\n\none thing i learned in the past , DONT QUIT STEAM WHILE IT IS UPDATING ,  i did this once ,like a month ago , and whenever i tried to start steam it would say ERROR in steam.dll or something like that .  i couldnt find a fix  and i had to uninstall and reinstall', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(273986, 43535, 1013, 102551, 1072936800, '', 'yea, well i never shut it down in the middle of an update, ive heard it messes with it. but i shut it down, restarted my computer like 3 times and its still giving me that message. anyone know what i can do?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(274119, 43535, 1013, 100027, 1072936800, '', 'Is Steam.exe marked as read-only?\n\n\n\nCan you check that you don\'t have a Steam.exe running (in the background maybe) before you try to run it?\n\n\n\nI\'m not sure what other reasons there would be for failing to delete that file.\n\n\n\nTaylor', '127.0.0.1', 1),
