(315460, 49456, 1013, 102610, 1072936800, '', '1.8 AMD XP\n\n512 MB DDR RAM PC2100\n\n80GB Hard Disk\n\n16X DVD ROM\n\n52X CD Writer\n\n15inch TFT Monitor\n\n64MB ATI Radeon 8500LE\n\nSoundblaster Live\n\n\n\nLooks ok doesnt it?, but it doesnt fookin work. Crashes after bout 20 mins of CS or an hours worth of listnin 2 MP3s, Added loads of case fans, everythin, and its complete [color=orange][font=wingdings]û[/font][/color][color=orange][font=wingdings]û[/font][/color][color=orange][font=wingdings]û[/font][/color][color=orange][font=wingdings]û[/font][/color]. CD writer wont read Cds nemore, i get cylic redundancy errors wen transferin stuff from my dvd drive. Looks like it cud b the Mobo but who knows?, so ur specs mayb ****, but specs mean nothin if a good spec doesnt work properly.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(315493, 49456, 1013, 102594, 1072936800, 'CPU', 'As this is already a silly thread I\'ll recount a little tale:\n\n\n\nI recently had problems with my computer over-heating. It transpires that the problem was due to the failure of the CPU cooling fan. To rectify the problem I determined to replace the fan. At first I was bewildered by startling array of choices on offer from many different vendors. However, I was able to narrow the field by virtue of the fact that most vendors clearly specify the the type of CPU each fan might be used for. After some further research I determined a range of model from which I could safely select a suitable fan. However, to my dismay I found I was presented  with yet another choice : the color of the light on the fan. Being somewhat perplexed by this I sought the advice of a local technical consultant - my 12 year old son. I explained to him that I was selecting a fan for my CPU (with a brief explanation of the need to cool the CPU) but I was presented with a choice of colors. Fortunately the vendor provides photos on a web site and we were able to review pictures of the offerings. After considering the option\'s he advised me that the fan with the blue light was definitely the best. When I asked him why, he answered that it was the \'coolest\' and therefore the most appropriate, since it was cooling that was the actual purpose was it not ? - I must admit I don\'t like it when he talks to me like I\'m stupid. Suspecting a mischievious play on words, I pressed him further on his reasons for making the choice. At first he appeared reluctant to explain further, but eventually recalled something about his art teacher explaining about blue being a cool colour. I ignored the ambiguity of that statement, and pondered the possibility that ambience of the color of the light might in some way affect the temperature of the CPU. I pointed out to him that the case was not transparent and the fan couldn\'t be seen. Nevertheless, he insisted that it was the \'coolest\' and therefore the most appropriate.\n\n\n\nI wondered if, in all the years I have spent in delivering and supporting computer systems, that I may have completely missed the point.\n\n\n\nSo I bought the one with the blue light. I can\'t see the light. It\'s inside the case, but occasionally I open it up to look inside and I have to agree with my son that it does look very cool...\n\n\n\n[]', '127.0.0.1', 1),
