(411305, 61587, 1013, 100028, 1072936800, 'Steam Startup and Connection Problem FAQ', 'For those of you having problems getting Steam to install or update\n\nand/or experiencing connection problems, here is a FAQ with a list of things to try.\n\nlast updated 16June2004 by qUiCkSiLvEr\n\n\n\nhttp://home.covad.net/~k25125/Steam...Problem_FAQ.htm (http://home.covad.net/~k25125/SteamyThings/Steam_Startup_Connection_Problem_FAQ.htm)\n\n\n\nThis is a work in progress and I\'ll be updating it with new information as I find it.\n\n\n\nMORE HELP FAQs HERE: qUiCkSiLvEr Help FAQs \n\n\n\nEdit by Taylor: your system clock no longer has to be the same as the Steam clock for Steam to work on your computer.\n\n\n\nGet this program and RUN IT:\n\n\n\nAtomicTime Program (http://schmail.com/atomictime/) <-- smallest Windows Time Program around!\n\n\n\nI\'ll be adding a troubleshooting flow chart and more detailed information about Steam and game port requirements and how to set up firewalls and routers to work with Steam over the next couple of weeks.\n\n\n\nIt\'s very important that if you try something out of the list and it works that you let me know so I can start adding emphasis to try those things first.\n\n\n\nHang in there everyone, stay positive.\n\n\n\nedit: and (lol) yes, I\'ll keep working on the color scheme!\n\n\n\nquote:Many systems are infected with spyware parasites that \n\ncan prevent Steam from installing/updating/running.For a quick check of your system go here:\n\nhttp://www.doxdesk.com/parasite/ (http://www.doxdesk.com/parasite/)\n\n\n\nTop Notes:\n\n\n\n1. Norton Script Blocking Can Keep Steam From Working!\n\n\n\n2.  MarketScore is not compatible with Steam!\n\n     Other hijacking proxy servers (spyware) are probably bad too.\n\n     here is a work-in-progress FAQ with specific removal help:\n\nMarketScore (Netsetter) Spyware Removal (http://home.covad.net/~k25125/fixes/MarketScore_Removal.htm)\n\n\n\n3.  Steganos Internet Anonym (IP masking proxy service) is not compatible with Steam!\n\n\n\n4.  Netlimiter is not compatible with Steam at this time.\n\nUpdate, -  NetLimiter 1.30 has been reported to work with Steam\n\n\n\n5.  CyberSitter has been identified to cause serious problems with Steam.\n\n\n\n6.   NetSonic Internet Accelerator or NetSonic Pro (Web3000) are not compatible with Steam.\n\n\n\n7. ActionTec modem/routers cause Problems!\n\nPlease read this Thread (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=95274) by ShUtY for specific information.\n\n\n\n8.  Only run One Firewall at a time.\n\n\n\n9.  Disabling a firewall is not necessarily removing it from your system!  If in doubt, uninstall it and re-install it.\n\n\n\n10.  If you are having problems, disable Steam from starting up when you boot until your problem is solved.\n\n\n\n11.  Trend Micro PC-cillin (anti-virus) includes a firewall!  Make sure you configure it to work with Steam.exe and HL.exe.\n\n\n\n12.  Make sure Steam.exe and HL.exe are NOT running in compatibility mode under XP.\n\n\n\n13. TweakMASTER DNS may cause problems with Steam.  PM me if you have this program.\n\n\n\n14. You can try exiting from Steam and deleting your ClientRegistry.blob file, especially if you have moved your Steam install.\n\n\n\n15. You can also try exiting from Steam and deleting platform.gcf\n\n\n\nAdditional Notes:\n\nAs a recommendation, if you are having problems, before doing a Steam Install or update, deactivate/disable any Anti-Virus or Internet Accelerator Programs as well as programs like AtGuard, NetZip or GetRight that may be running.\n\n\n\nIt is possible (from what I\'m seeing) that some versions of these programs can interfere with the Steam Install/Update  process.\n\n\n\nIf you see problems and one of these type of programs is running, they may very well be the cause of the failures you are seeing.\n\n\n\n*==================================*\n\n* Here is a summary of what I\'m seeing so far *\n\n*==================================*quote:Steam can either communicate on the internet or it can\'t.\n\nIf it can\'t then something is preventing it from doing so. The known (big) offenders are:\n\n1.   Firewalls that aren\'t properly configured\n\n2.   proxy servers\n\n3.   trojans or virus\' that scramble the executable code\n\n4.   Spyware programs that interfere with normal internet activity\n\n5.   Anonymous IP programs (another proxy program really)\n\n6.   incompletely uninstalled firewall programs\n\n7.   multiple running firewall programs\n\n8.   multiple running anti-virus programs\n\n9.   programs that are intended to block spam but aren\'t set up right.\n\n10.  download accelerators (again, another proxy program)\n\n11.  some browser toolbar addons\n\n12.  multiple network interface cards in one computer\n\n13.  P2P programs like Kazaa that are blocking a significant amount of the downlink or uplink bandwidth so Steam transactions get seriously delayed or even lost.\n\n14. Other computers on a home network that are utilizing a significant amount of the downlink or uplink bandwidth causing Steam transactions to get seriously delayed or lost. 15. The player\'s dsl/ISDN/cable/telephone line is marginal.\n\n16. The Player\'s computer has a O/S configuration problem causing IRQ/DMA/memory conflicts\n\n17.  The Player\'s computer actually has bad memory or some other electronic problem.\n\n\n\nYou can d/l a very good free memory test program from here:\n\nSimmtester Free DocMeM Memory Test Program (http://www.simmtester.com/)\n\nYou have to register (free, no spam from them either) to get the program.\n\n\n\nIt makes a bootable floppy that will then test your system memory very very thoroughly ... it may take several hours if you have a lot of memory or a slow machine.\n\n\n\nIn addition, Internet Connection Sharing Software can also be a problem\n\n(again another form of proxy).\n\n\n\nThere are other things to look for but these are the top things I\'ve found so far.\n\n\n\nThings that can cause problems that are somewhat out of player\'s control are:\n\n1.  Steam network overloaded\n\n2.  Player\'s ISP having problems\n\n3.  Content provider is overloaded (this is related but independent from Steam itself)\n\n4.  Player\'s ISP is intentionally blocking certain ports for security reasons (many school and university backbones)\n\n5.  The internet itself is overloaded due to some trojan or virus propagating\n\n\n\nHaving System Resources Problems? Here is a good guide on what you can enable and disable (WinXP Home/Pro):How to free up Windows XP system resources (http://service1.symantec.com/SUPPORT/tsgeninfo.nsf/pfdocs/2002010712042239) and if you are adventurous you can try some of these tweaks too: BlackViper Tweaks (http://www.blackviper.com/)\n\n\n\nHave a Modem? then check out:\n\n Dr Tweak\'s Advanced 56k help guide (found by w00ters) (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=73708)\n\nOne think I want to say here though, download managers are important for Modem users but there are also many bad download managers/accelerators that may interfere with Steam, please see item #11 above.\n\n\n\nFor those of you with Mouse or Keyboard Problems, try this thread:\n\nw00ter\'s Mouse Lag Guide (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=76551)\n\n\n\nI\'ll continue to add to these lists over time and add specific solutions if Players will continue to submit good error descriptions and their msinfo32 files.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(414016, 62041, 1005, 100076, 1072936800, 'Installation of custom maps and add/remove programs list', 'I play custom maps in DoD quite a bit. I generally download these maps seperately and adjust the auto installer install directory accordingly to get the files in the right place. \n\n\n\nRecently, however, I was going through my add/remove programs list and found 2 listings. One for each of the maps I had most recently installed. I don\'t really see this as unusual until I notice that no other maps are listed AND there size is 1,528.00MB each! Then I look at Steam and it is rated at over 3,000MB.\n\n\n\nCould someone theorize on what is going on here? Is it just DoD and Counter-Strike installs with incorrect names? Is it something else. These programs listings are WAY too big to be the maps I installed (obviously).\n\n\n\nEDIT: I would also like to add that this has been reported by at least 1 other person who has virtually the same issue. That said, check your program listings and see what it says. Does it look the same?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(414095, 62041, 1005, 103423, 1072936800, '', 'well, some self-installers create uninstallers.\n\n\n\nSo, when you feel like uninstalling some maps, you could easily just run the uninstaller and it\'d delete those specific maps.\n\n\n\nNow, the file-size. The file size that shows on the add/remove program thingie gets its number from the FOLDER in which the program (or file) is installed. The map itself is probably only about 5 megs big. But your maps folder is most likely 1,528 MBs. (1.5 gigs, roughly). That is why both maps show the same size, because they (and their uninstallers) are located in the same directory. Also, you\'ll notice weird file-sizes on other programs. Some games will only show sizes like "327 kbs" or "4,300 kbs" when in reality the game takes up 2-3 gigs of space. That\'s caused of the same reason. The uninstaller is probably placed in a seperate folder within the game\'s folder, so it doesn\'t read the rest of the material, thus creating a small size (whatever\'s in the folder it\'s in) ... get the drift?\n\n\n\nSteam is, in fact, over 3 gigs big. That\'s just the way steam is, lol...\n\n\n\nSo, those two maps are not sucking up all your space... just your custom maps and steam in general are! =b', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(417392, 62500, 1013, 102768, 1072936800, 'Random System Crashes while using steam/cs', 'I have been getting random system freezes while using steam/counter-strike.  There is no prelude, and the freezes are random and cannot be reproduced.  They happend 2-3 times in a two hour period.\n\n\n\nWindows XP\n\n1 gig ram\n\nradeon 9600 card with omega drivers (latest)\n\n\n\nThis has been going on since the previous update and was not corrected with the newest update.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(417805, 62500, 1013, 102769, 1072936800, '', 'I\'ve used the Omegas for maybe 20 minutes, total time, so I dunno whether it\'s possible that they\'re causing some sort of conflict.  Seems somewhat unlikely, though, considering that lots of people use them.\n\n\n\nDo you have anything overclocked?  Processor?  Video card?\n\n\n\nAny other problems with having a laggy feel in Windows or those system lockups when NOT in steam/CS?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(419716, 62500, 1013, 102768, 1072936800, '', 'None of my hardware is overclocked and these problems do not effect any other software.  The freezes are extremly quick and come without any warning whatsoever.  I am unable to ctr+alt+delete to get out and am forced to restart my computer.\n\n\n\nI run antivirus once a week and disinfect spyware once a week as well.\n\n\n\nThis problem began with the steam update before the last bigger one.\n\n\n\nI am going to try different video drivers, but I really hate to do so.  These Omega drivers really are amazing, makes desert combat run MUCH smoother (it also doesnt lock up every half hour when running desert combat).', '127.0.0.1', 1),
