(532758, 76551, 1013, 102929, 1072936800, '', 'EXCELLENT post on input lag: /forums/...2400#post532400 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&postid=532400#post532400)\n\n\n\nVERY long and tough to sort through it all... a little duplicate data but a lot of NEW and USEFUL informaiton.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(535632, 76551, 1013, 102929, 1072936800, '', 'Dick ^Bump^kis', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(622978, 76551, 1013, 102930, 1072936800, '', 'Bump... Problem still exists.  Mouse guide was a good bandaid.  Made it semi playable.  I can play sniper or mg that doesn\'t require as much mouse movement. Thank you for taking the time.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(622989, 76551, 1013, 100220, 1072936800, '', 'Installing drivers for my Logitech optical mouse just messed stuff up. My sensitivity was still higher than my liking, even when both the system sensitivity and in-game sensitivty were both at their lowest values.\n\n\n\nAn audio process (CTHELPER.EXE) sometimes consumes a large amount of memory and is usually the sole cause for mouse lag with my computer.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(623084, 76551, 1013, 102931, 1072936800, '', 'The mouse issue is if the mouse just sucks....... simple....', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(623459, 76551, 1013, 102930, 1072936800, '', 'Everyday I hear another player complaining about it just starting to happen.  It\'s spreading like a virus.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(623757, 76551, 1013, 100392, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by Master_America \n\nInstalling drivers for my Logitech optical mouse just messed stuff up. My sensitivity was still higher than my liking, even when both the system sensitivity and in-game sensitivty were both at their lowest values.\n\n\n\nAn audio process (CTHELPER.EXE) sometimes consumes a large amount of memory and is usually the sole cause for mouse lag with my computer. \n\nIf youre mouse sens is too high, try adding -noforcemspd string to the launch options field box.\n\n\n\nCTHELPER.EXE: This is installed with the SoundBlaster Audigy2 drivers. It takes about 4 MB of RAM.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(623770, 76551, 1013, 102160, 1072936800, '', 'another possibility if usin windows xp powertoy taskswith (or the taskmanager replacement tool).\n\nyour controlls can get verry verry messed up and laggy with it.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(623780, 76551, 1013, 100392, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by �W- \n\nanother possibility if usin windows xp powertoy taskswith (or the taskmanager replacement tool).\n\nyour controlls can get verry verry messed up and laggy with it. \n\nThx u very much but that is in the "other suggestions" portion of the guide. Plz read through the other suggestions if the guide doesnt fix the problem. If you come up w/ anything new, post it here so I can include it in this guide.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(524693, 76787, 1013, 103428, 1072936800, 'Possible fix for microphone input changing (livedrive problem)', 'I\'ve had the problem which I think is described here:\n\n/forums/show...;threadid=13208 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=13208)\n\n/forums/show...;threadid=43849 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=43849)\n\n/forums/show...;threadid=24977 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=24977)\n\n/forums/show...;threadid=57348 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=57348)\n\n/forums/show...;threadid=53560 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=53560)\n\n/forums/show...;threadid=11287 (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=11287)\n\n\n\nwhere steam games switch my recording line (in my case, line-in) to microphone every time they are loaded/change maps. With a bit of poking around the windows mixer APIs, I made a tiny tool that should help people suffering from this problem, removing the need to alt-tab to switch your settings back. It is located here:\n\n\n\nhttp://fa.glassfish.net/mixerfixer.zip (http://fa.glassfish.net/mixerfixer.zip)\n\n\n\nBasically what it does is reset the recording input device to a value you specify at a time interval (say every 30 seconds or so, but you can tell it to do it faster/slower).\n\n\n\nNote that I do not have an SB Live or any such other card. I have a Santa Cruz Turtle Beach. So if this does not work for you (and you are sure you are setting it correctly), please copy the full output of the program and e-mail it to me. It works for me just fine though.\n\n\n\nIt was also a very quick utility to program, and looking at the dates on those threads, I\'m surprised Valve hasn\'t fixed such a glaring issue after all these months. It really just takes a few lines of code once you have the mixer device open... I thought steam was supposed to encourage these kind of quick fixes?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(524751, 76787, 1013, 103429, 1072936800, '', 'it works!\n\n\n\nnice one []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525847, 76787, 1013, 102682, 1072936800, '', 'It\'s pathetic that Valve havn\'t fixed it themselves, you\'re right. Even more so because it was a problem they themselves introduced with Steam and 1.6. It also saddens me to learn that it only took you a few hours to do - even with little knowledge on the subject. And yet this has been a problem literally thousands of Steam users have had and Valve have done absolutely NOTHING to address it in 8 MONTHS.\n\n\n\nThankyou very much for the fix glassfish []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525866, 76787, 1013, 103428, 1072936800, '', 'no problem []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525949, 76787, 1013, 102716, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by glassfish \n\nno problem []  i dont have this problem but good job man u shoudl work for valve [](now u need to ask them to distribute the fix over steam [] )', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526097, 76787, 1013, 103430, 1072936800, '', 'Excellent Tool, I shall try it out.\n\n\n\nBy the way, if Valve read this, thanks for neglecting all those people out there with this problem that someone else was soo kind enough to help out...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526304, 76787, 1013, 100106, 1072936800, '', 'thank you for the workaround!\n\nbut /me is still waiting for a official fix!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526661, 76787, 1013, 103431, 1072936800, '', 'Omg. It works. I have been forced to use my onboard sound for microphone because Steam always changed the recording source to something else, but this little programs works. Thank you glassfish!! Great work!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526718, 76787, 1013, 102900, 1072936800, '', 'Always love a GUI but this program gets the job done perfectly and is as lightweight as a workaround can possibly be. Great work! I tried it with my Audigy 2 and it worked just fine.\n\n\n\nI have a theory on why Valve has left this behavior in Steam unfixed. Do any of you remember when voice communication was first added to HL in that one update? Remember what option they gave you if you didn\'t have a headset? Buy a Plantronics headset! More specifically... a USB headset like the DSP-500 (they even make/made? one branded for CS). Well guess who actually owns that headset and never has problems in Steam? Me. The mixer for this headset is very basic as it only has a playback and a recording volume control in the mixer. When I plug it in, it takes over as the default sound card and when I go into Steam, it is set as the default mic. That is just one theory that, to me, seems to make sense. I could be totally off here. It could be that Valve just decided that everyone plugs their mic into the mic jack and anyone who is different doesn\'t matter...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526784, 76787, 1013, 103428, 1072936800, '', 'I apologize for the lack of a gui. I do most of my programming in UNIX and as such I am not completely familiar with the windows API, etc.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526803, 76787, 1013, 102900, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by glassfish \n\nI apologize for the lack of a gui. I do most of my programming in UNIX and as such I am not completely familiar with the windows API, etc.  \n\n\n\nNo problem at all! I\'m a Linux guy myself, so the program actually did seem to give me that feel. Besides, a GUI would probably just waste resources anyways. []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(590156, 76787, 1013, 103432, 1072936800, '', 'Works great! Thanks for the fix.\n\n\n\nThis got me thinking it would be nice if I could make an app that allowed you to change settings in the Record Control when you pressed and then released a key on your keyboard.\n\nI\'m pretty inexperienced, but it would be fun to try it.\n\n\n\nI just want to know how hard it was for you?\n\n\n\nThanks again,\n\nEJ', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(596363, 76787, 1013, 103433, 1072936800, 'using a headset', 'is it possible to have the voice output routed to headset, and the rest of the sound go to my speakers?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(598618, 76787, 1013, 103434, 1072936800, '', 'nifty program, almost worked for me =/ except that when I run it and connect to game i\'m told in the HL Console "my mic has been disabled due to something using it in the background" or something along those lines. It\'s same message I get when I run any 3rd party voice communication software ie. TeamSpeak ect and try connecting to any HL Mod. I can only use one Voice Communication type program at a time (no onboard sound) and I\'m running SoundBlaster Live! Platnium, any suggested fixes for this? This whole mic switching inputs issue has been haunting me since I was forced to convert to Steam =/ I really wish valve or whoever could fix these glitches maybe even in the next PATCH!?. I had to buy a freaking extension cord and connector just so I could plug my mic into the back (primary) input', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(621670, 76787, 1013, 103281, 1072936800, '', 'Maybe they made this bug to prevent people from playing music over voicecom? []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525015, 76817, 1013, 102935, 1072936800, 'Bugs and errors reporting', 'Greetings!\n\n\n\nWandering around on the forum, I noticed there is a specific thread for reporting encountered bugs and errors. Though this may be suitable in a sufficient way, do you not think it would be easier if all reporting of such errors would be centralised and consultable in a single database? In my opinion, this would be better for several reasons, those being:\n\n\n\nbetter organisation of bugs (with status for each bug, such as: �Critical\', �Important\', �Normal\', �Minimal\', �Resolved\');\n\neasier consultation of posted bugs;\n\neasier and less cluttered research of posted bugs (to avoid duplicate postings, for example);\n\nto a certain extent, easier moderation;\n\npossible further discussion of each posted bug individually;\n\nintegration with Steam forum account also possible.\n\n\n\nThis can be quite easily done (with PHP, and MySQL), and if done well could make following of bugs and errors easier. I am imagining the interface could resemble that of Mozilla Bugzilla (http://bugzilla.mozdev.org/) (with this list interface (http://bugzilla.mozilla.org/buglist.cgi?product=Browser,MailNews& bug_status=UNCONFIRMED,NEW,ASSIGNED,REOPENED,RESOL\n\nVED&chfield=%5BBug%20creation%5D&chfieldfrom=-0d)) for example (of course, without Bugzilla\'s specific complications). Thank you for your answers on this subject.\n\n\n\nThomas.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525027, 76817, 1013, 100566, 1072936800, '', 'This really should belong in the Suggestions & Ideas forum, but who am I to complain. ^_^\n\n\n\nYeah this sounds like a great idea though. And that list looks just like what Steam needs!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525041, 76817, 1013, 102935, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by Clush \n\nThis really should belong in the Suggestions & Ideas forum Sorry about that, but the bugs reporting thread is in this forum and I did not notice the Suggestions & Ideas forum. Perhaps the latter is only for actual Steam suggestions, and not forum suggestions � but I\'ll let a moderator see to that.\n\n\n\nI am pleased you are appreciative of the idea, I think it really could make bugs reporting that much more easy and organised (which is always a plus). I\'m currently working on a script lateral to a vBulletin forum so I could integrate bugs reporting to the forum account (if I were chosen to accomplish the task of writing this, of course).', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525045, 76817, 1013, 100566, 1072936800, '', 'Hehe sorry I didn\'t totally mean it, just that everyone else says it, why can\'t I! [] Ahh you\'re working on a script? Sweet! I\'d love to see how it\'s getting along sometime.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525063, 76817, 1013, 100511, 1072936800, '', 'I have made an psudo-campaigne about getting better bug reporting for Steam.  I\'ve layed out a number of ideas in this thread (/forums/showthread.php?s=&threadid=72426) and just the other day I PMed Waldo about what possible options are for better bug reporting (he has not yet responded).', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525088, 76817, 1013, 102935, 1072936800, '', 'I understand your idea, but I am thinking of a separate, specialised interface specifically for bug reporting (please see the link to the list interface in my initial post to have an idea of the kind of interface I am imagining would be adequate for the system). This would allow not only for the bug postings to be more specific (with system specifications, for example) but also for the organisation to be easier for both users and moderators.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525133, 76817, 1013, 100511, 1072936800, '', 'I concur with the Buzilla-ish interface (and included a mention to it in my PM to Waldo), but I am concerned about the potential abuse of the system.  The current Bug Report thread is plagued by flames, inaccurate bugs or inappropriate bugs (things that are not really Steam bugs).  I do understand the inherent organizational improvement that such a system would bring and perhaps that is a good first step, but I am looking toward creating a bug reporting community that is competent and dedicated and most of all, effective and efficient.  A new interface would defiantly help with this, but I am aiming at a new community; one shielded from the flak of unwashed masses.\n\n\n\nBut perhaps I am overreacting.  A bugzilla-like system would, as you mention, make it easier for mods to delete frivolous information.  Perhaps a drastic social overhaul (such as the one I\'m suggesting) is not needed.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525162, 76817, 1013, 102935, 1072936800, '', 'In my opinion, the organisational improvement such a system could also bring would be that the bug listing would be isolated from other posts and in this sense problems within the bug listing could be noticed more easily, and hence dealt with faster and more efficiently. Since a separate table for the status of each forum member could be created and updated progressively. This would allow for several improvements upon the traditional bug reporting method, such as:\n\n\n\nmoderators only for the bug listing and not for the forums (and vice-versa, if the need be an issue);\n\nwarnings and bans could be issued to trouble-makers on the bug listing, without effect on the users\' forum account.\n\n\n\nThis way, the bug listing could prove to be a much more efficient and practical way to enlist and index bugs � open to all users with a Steam forum account � while maintaining a customisable level of protection against eventual problems that could arise (with flamebait, etc...).', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525523, 76817, 1013, 100511, 1072936800, '', 'Vexware, I think you have the solution.  So... how do we make it happen?\n\n\n\nOption 1\n\nConvince Valve to spend the time on the system.  This would require some well-phrased e-mails to the right people (Waldo, Erik, and Taylor come first to mind) and a good deal of luck.\n\n\n\nOption 2\n\nSpend the time to make the system for Valve.  I don\'t envision Valve ever allowing this, but it is worth a hypothetical mention (considering that they may be too busy to make the system themselves).\n\n\n\nOption 3\n\nSpend the time to make our own system, independent of Steampowered.com\'s forums.  Again, this is a hypothetical solution if Valve doesn\'t have the time.  It would require a group of entrepreneur bug-trackers to register a domain, set up the system and actively maintain a high-quality bug database.  The pros are that we could be as discriminating as we want and we can have complete control over the system\'s capabilities.  The cons are the lack of integration with the Steam forums, the potential lack of integration with Valve (no progress information) and to possibility that Valve would ignore us altogether, though I highly doubt that (as long as we have good bug-reporting).  It is worth mentioning that, if we go this route, we might find some help from Valve in the form of server space (if we asked very, very nicely).\n\n\n\nOption 4\n\nPublish an "official" bug list.  If we cannot convince Valve to make such a system and we cannot make one ourselves, we could create a private bug-tracking group and publish our list of bugs however often is required.  If we register our own site and make a precedent of reliably reporting significant bugs in a professional manner, it is very likely that Valve will give a serious look at any bugs we report.  The pros are that all of the relevant bugs are coalesced into one, easy-to-read document and there is a good chance we could quickly gather the respect of the community and Valve.  The major con is the closed nature of the group.  The group members are not likely to find every bug to be found and this would necessitate scouring the Steam forums for other bugs.  We could also set up an e-mail address for the general community to mail in their bugs for inclusion in the list.  If option 1 is not possible, I see this as being the next best idea in terms of being realistically doable.\n\n\n\nThese are the solutions I have.  Please respond with reactions, alternatives, or amendments.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525581, 76817, 1013, 100566, 1072936800, '', 'Lunchtimemama,\n\n\n\nAll perfectly workable options, but I think that maybe you should develop your own system first to test it out and improve on it before pouncing it on Valve. \n\n\n\nJust a thought, so please entertain me on this, but what about a bug submission form at the top (like by Status or whatever) and then developing a mod to install into the board which pulls down the moderated submissions into a forum, so that people can check it to see if the bug has been fixed, or being fixed etc.\n\n\n\nIf you guys need some help, I\'ll try and lend a helping hand where ever possible! PM meh for contact details. []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525596, 76817, 1013, 102935, 1072936800, '', 'In my opinion, there are already there two options of which we may omit consideration: the first and fourth options. I think we can forget about the first option for several reasons, the most important being that it would be both time-consuming to send several e-mails and waiting for the (probably negative) answers and that these important people will most probably decline our proposition to have them write the system due to their important workload. Also, I believe we may forget the fourth option, since I have the skills (in PHP and MySQL) necessary to write such a system. With the omission of the first and fourth options, let\'s consider the intermediate propositions.\n\n\n\nOption 2\n\nThis is the path I was rather thinking of, because as I have said, I do have the skills to write such a script and have it integrated to the already existing Steam web platform. I am hoping that we can get some attention from the Steam web team, and subsequently enter a frame of negociation with the team about the integration of the bug listing project to the Steam website.\n\n\n\nOption 3\n\nThis is a path we could consider should the previous option not be a success. The obvious disadvantages of this solution are of course the inexistence of integration to the already existing Steam web platform, and the eventual lack of attention from the Steam web team itself. We could of course create a community-driven website, but I think this is running a risk as perhaps the Steam team would ignore the site and instead continue concentrating on the input in the Steam forum.\n\n\n\nIdeally, the best option would be the second one you listed, because this would mean full support from the Steam web team and officialisation of the resource, so it would be considered a serious bugs database. Whichever the option, I hone the skills needed to create such a system to that is not where we need to focus; we should rather put the limelight on attracting the Steam web team\'s attention by showing our motivation and our capability in producing such a system and demonstrating how it improves upon the bugs reporting scheme already in place. For this, we first need to start by writing a basic version of the script and presenting them the test version so they can make up an opinion of their own.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525597, 76817, 1013, 100511, 1072936800, '', 'Perhaps we could first execute Option 4 (while Option 3 is being developed), then move to Option 3, then, upon it\'s success, integrate our system with Valves and finally arrive at Option 2.\n\n\n\nWhat do you think?\n\n\n\nNOTE:  Clush, check your PMs.  Also, anyone else interested in bug-reporting-reform, feel free to PM me with contact info as well.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525604, 76817, 1013, 100566, 1072936800, '', 'Vexware, both you and Lunchtimemama have some solid ideas, and I can see there\'s a good chance creating a system would work.\n\n\n\nVexware, are you a part-time/full-time/hobby programmer? Not questioning your skills or anything! BTW check your PM in a minute or so.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(525632, 76817, 1013, 102935, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by Clush \n\nVexware, are you a part-time/full-time/hobby programmer? Not questioning your skills or anything! BTW check your PM in a minute or so.  I currently program more as a hobbyist than as a professional, though I am aiming to get into the scope of professional programming later on (more specifically in the field of video games).', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(526395, 76817, 1013, 100566, 1072936800, '', 'Anyone else liking this idea?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(530418, 77478, 1013, 102936, 1072936800, 'Want Free Cheats?', 'I am running 3 CS servers \n\n\n\nI am also running 1 VAC Secure server.\n\n\n\nTotal= 4 CS SERVERS\n\n\n\nI have 3 servers falsely dedicated as a cheat server [NON-SECURE], all redirecting to a VAC secure server!\n\n\n\nThe reasults:\n\n\n\nWhen re-directed to the VAC secure server, I keep seeing players getting banned, in console via HLSW for violations.\n\n\n\nHehe...That will teach em not to cheat!\n\n\n\nI planned to expend more servers!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(530424, 77478, 1013, 102937, 1072936800, '', 'I dont care too much for cheaters either, but thats not exactly fair play.  People that openly want to play on cheat servers, with other cheats should be allowed to do so if that\'s what floats thier boat.  Geez man, who do you think you are?  The equalizer or something?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
