(870895, 110442, 1013, 100373, 1072936800, 'Newbie Questions', 'Hello Mr Taylor Sir,\n\nI am a new member and so have a few questions.....\n\n\n\n1: I have got steam but haven\'t installed any games yet so I haven\'t put my ATI key in anywhere yet. Does this mean that I just update my steam to the new steam when it\'s out and enter my key in the appropriate box?\n\n\n\n2: Is there anyway to backup the games such as half life 2 or to backup saved games at all?\n\n\n\n3: Do you have to be logged into steam all the time to play HL2 or can I play it offline?\n\n\n\n4: Any idea roughly how long it will take to download HL2 and CS Source over a 512K ADSL broadband connection?\n\n\n\n5: Any idea\'s yet as to the release dates of HL2 and CS Source Beta on steam?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(871894, 110442, 1013, 100027, 1072936800, '', '1. You can wait until HL2 comes out and enter it then, or you can enter it now - click Browse Games, then click Register a Product I Already Own. It will accept the ATI key -- and though it looks like nothing has changed, unless you see an error message, the key is on your account and HL2 will show up in My Games when it\'s released.\n\n\n\n2. sure - you can backup the entire SteamApps directory if you want. Or, you can just backup the config files and save games (in the SAVE folders), and/or the Steam cache files (.gcf - where most of the data lives).\n\n\n\n3. You will be able to play HL2 offline. However, to do this, you first have to Login to Steam "online" -- to go Offline, Steam requires that the last time you ran it you were logged in, so that it can be sure that you own the games you are trying to play. So, if you use Steam "online", the game is at 100% loaded, and you choose "Exit" and NOT "Exit and Logout", you should then be able to launch Steam when not connected, and play.\n\n\n\n4, 5: sorry, don\'t have an answer for either of these yet.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(872009, 110442, 1013, 100373, 1072936800, 'Reply', 'Oh right  Cool  ,so I can wipe my machine which I tend to do now and then as windows is sooo good with it\'s reliable registry getting full up with crap, that I can reinstall steam then reactivate my account and then put all the backup steam files into the new folders and it\'ll be just like old, and I take it that the steam servers keep my cd key and account details so it can be simply reactivated.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(872271, 110442, 1013, 100370, 1072936800, '', 'Hi, a while ago, I entered my Platinum Pack key into Steam, then bought CZ, and I just input my ATI key. In total, I have the ATI Key, the Platinum key, and CZ. Would it be possible to transfer the Platinum key to my brothers account? Since I have no need for them both? Or, did the Platinum key get overwritten. Or, must I mail in the platinum cd sleeve? Thanks.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(872600, 110442, 1013, 100027, 1072936800, '', 'Sorry, the keys cannot be transferred. If you mailed in the PlatPack key, the person processing it would just email you back and tell you that it was already registered to your own account.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(897098, 110442, 1013, 100374, 1072936800, '', 'Question for Taylor:\n\n\n\nI purchased an ATI card with the HL2 voucher last October. \n\nI sent in the paperwork to get the HL2 game on CD when it comes out. This was before the Premier Pack was offered.\n\n\n\nA few months later I was mailed the Valve Premier Pack disk and I am currently using STEAM with the original HL2 voucher code entered, but I still want to receive  HL2 on CD when it is released. Is it possible for me to still get the game on CD since I am using STEAM with the voucher code?\n\n\n\nIn addition, I have since moved from the address that was submitted along with the ATI voucher and I have changed email address.   I\'m not sure how I will be contacted (other than STEAM) when HL2 is released.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(897219, 110442, 1013, 100363, 1072936800, '', 'yes, I am also wondering is it possible for me to get Hl2 from retail on CD if I have already entered my Voucher Key into Steam? Becuase I want it on CD not on steam []\n\noldfaq', '127.0.0.1', 1),
