(1543236, 177559, 1040, 101646, 1072936800, '', 'BUMP\n\n\n\n\n\nThat\'s a good call about the soldier about oposing force.  However, I think much fewer people would know anything or really care about what happens to him.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543488, 177559, 1040, 101652, 1072936800, '', 'I know what you mean, Great game and story. But it seem like they couldnt be botherd towards the end and stuck a crap ending on that hasent explained anything. Damn i waited years for this.\n\nIts to short i think too.\n\n\n\nBut other than that, Great game:-)', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543575, 177559, 1040, 101653, 1072936800, '', 'Spoiler:\n\nThe strategy guide explains a little bit to clear things up for the combine. The combine is an alien race (I hope I read right) and came from another dimension. They took over most of Earth and made a deal to protect the people of Earth with a bargain Wallace Brien made with them. In HL1, Gordon is suspended in some sort of dark void to be preserved for more jobs (for instance, maybe killing off part of the combine?). So at the end of HL2, The G-man then represerves Gordon again for yet another time...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543600, 177559, 1040, 101654, 1072936800, '', 'Spoiler: \n\nOkay Half Life ended with the G Man - no choice really - work for the G Man or die - so HL2 starts with Gordon waking up to smell the ashes - we presume this has led on from the Aliens infilltrating the planet via the Black Mesa Disaster \n\n\n\n  Right at the start as you wake up the guy says - Hello didnt see you get on - So you either teleported in or were put there \n\n\n\n   This doesnt really explain why or how Barney and co are expecting you \n\n\n\n    Then the end - the G Man can stop time - freeze the action suspend Gordon and hold him again till the next episode \n\n\n\n   Now one thread is that the G Man is part of a larger government/alien conspiracy that wanted Black Mesa to happen - as a contigency plan they reanimate Gordon Freeman to solve the conbine issue with City 17 \n\n\n\n   But as a more simpler idea - no - G Man is simply the games programmer - he runs the show he makes the worlds - and lets You aka Gordon Freeman - play his games - until you get so far - but he wont let you win the games - wont let you get alyx in the shack and win at the end - no He`ll keep you there suspended in animation again until he reanimates you for HL3 \n\n\n\n   Hes seen at the start of and during both HL and Hl2 - play it again youll see the G Man a few times make a sneak appearance \n\n\n\n     Overall I thought it was pretty weak - its trying to be clever but in effect weakens any sense out of the idea - they would have been better rewarding us with a escape from the conbine sequence at the end and have gordon knocked out \n\n\n\n    Basically its a teaser - leaving it open for various interpretations - and I think whatever comes out - Valve will nick and use to feed HL3 []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1541806, 178246, 1040, 101655, 1072936800, 'Hl1/hl2 story so far..', 'For those of you especially who think the story is weak:\n\n\n\n*don\'t read unless you beat both, or don\'t care to spoil the ending.. you may want to read it when you finish hl2. There are no cliffs, so dont ask, and i dont care if you dont read it*\n\n\n\nAs we all know, Gordon doesn\'t talk. This was a design feature, so that you know what he knows and he knows what you know. Especially in HL2 where you are dropped in the middle of a world with ©©©© going on you have no clue on. The only reason we even have reason to believe it is Earth, is because all your old friends are there, and no one mentions "traveling to another world." The thing I love about this story, is because (now I\'m playing hl2 a second time) I notice things the second time around I missed completely, same with the first, and now that I\'m play hl1 source, I\'m noticing a few other things as well.\n\n\n\nHl1 starts out with you in the infamous tram. As you near the destination, some information is revealed to you about Gordon. MIT Graduate in theoretical physics, your clearance, and your sponser, who is unknown. Weird as that may be, when HL2 starts, gman says "The right man in the wrong place can make a world of difference." and even though at the end of HL1 he says "I like a survivor, reminds me of myself." It SOUNDS like he didn\'t know you, but WHY do you see him all the time in both games? Following you. Not to mention near the end of HL2 the Administrator says something about how as a baby, you were given something (I can\'t remember right now) that explains why a mere scientist can blow up entire human/alien/combine armies, but still not completely clear.\n\n\n\nAnyway, the sponser wouldn\'t surpirse me at all, if it turned out to be Gman. He anonomously sponsers you to work at black mesa, cuz he KNOWS ©©©© is going to go down.\n\n\n\nNow, why were they trying to get aliens in other dimensions in the first place? Well in the first, you kill the Nihilath, which controls them all. Therefor leading them all leaderless. I would think the administrator wanted this, as in the second you see many vortgornauts (sp?) used as slaves. The dude with the broom right as you get off the train.\n\n\n\nWhen you get to black mesa east, in HL2, as you descend the elevator, there are Vort\'s cooking (your friends, clearly.. perhaps they like humans cuz you saved them from being slaves to the nihilath.) headcrabs. Then later, at ravenholm, you hear the priest say "I must tend to my flock." It may be possible, after a time, they brought over the aliens for food (Maybe something to do with humans using all their resources on earth, so we looked for alternatives). Also, clearly, they use them as weapons when "shelling" an area.\n\n\n\nAs for the Gman, something tells me he knew you before Black Mesa. Something also tells me he is not only fully aware of what is going to happen, but also, perhaps he holds a greater purpose in this war.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.phenylshouse.com/members...oast_030000.jpg (http://www.phenylshouse.com/members/ritalin/Pictures/HL2/d2_coast_030000.jpg)\n\n\n\nIn that pic on the coast. You are looking through a combine binocular set up. Though it seems like an ordinary "Hey, the gman is there" situation. I zoomed in on that pic (try it yourself) he is talk to the colonol (the dude with the british accent who gives you the RPG) Check out the leet toque on his head. I\'m sure it is him. So, then you drive over to that town and everyone says "We are expecting a Gunship attack." Now how the ©©©© do they know that? Could be a few ways, intercepted radio transmissions, or maybe the Gman said it. Maybe the Gman SOLD the RPG to them (Perhaps he makes profit on war. As he says at the end of the game, "I have numerous employers who want to buy you." or whatever.) Also, when going on the underground railway (in the canel) you drop down into a broken down rail cart, with a Vort and a Resistance member. If you are quick enough, you can see the Vort, by the TV, is watching the Gman... Why? ©©©© if I know.\n\n\n\nAnyway, when you are in nova prospekt and teleport out, and then, even though, your travel instantly to the Dr. Klieners Lab, he says it\'s been a week. I heard some moron on another board say "It\'s because they couldn\'t figure out how to start the rebellion, and wanted to get the game out." Well, maybe, but I figure it\'s to prove that the teleporters dont only transport you, but can go through time. Though, kliener says it\'s a "Slow teleport", perhaps it is an accelerated teleport. Maybe thats why gordon went straight from blackmesa, to city17 without anything in between, even though its been about 10 years.\n\n\n\nHow do I know it has to be about 10 years? When you get to Eli Vance, in Black Mesa East, and you can check out a picture of him, his wife, and Alyx. In that pic, she looks about 8-10. (Maybe younger) and Eli says "Ahh, you remember my wife don\'t you? That was the only thing I could save from Black Mesa". Well clearly Alyx is no little girl anymore.. and is at LEAST 20, if not older. So yes, a fair bit of time has passed.\n\n\n\nAs for the combine, what are they? I noticed while inside the citadel, there were "Mechanics" of some sort. Looked humanish, but with legs (shorter) like striders (little stick legs). The Administrator is also constantly talking about immortality and "Evolution". It SOUNDS like he is doing something to the captured humans to accelerate evolution. Being the combine? Could be.. I wouldn\'t doubt those Engineer\'s seen working on ©©©© in the Citadel, have their own Combine uniform.\n\n\n\nNow for the "Benefactor\'s". When you do the teleport at the beginning, the admin says "Who are you? How did you get here?" You teleport away. When you come back you see him talking on a Video thing, with .. something, saying "I Think it was Gordon Freeman" or whatever. Well, later in the game, when you sneak up on him, he is talking to that "thing" again. It looks alien, maybe a robot? I\'m thinking when he says he is going to teleport to another "Universe" that is where he is going and that is who the "Benefactors" are, and they are the one\'s who gave him the citadel, and who gave him this power over everyone.\n\n\n\nAs for all the people, you notice a few people you talk to mention "Scientists" and when you get to the "Sandtrap" chapter, one guy lies by his dead friend saying "Why? He was the finest mind of this age". They are capturing all the bright minds to use them to evolve to greater beings to create a master race? Maybe.\n\n\n\nLastly, the Gman is always seen everywhere, and disapears. Even at dead ends, he more than likely teleports somewhere else. He aslo, as you see in the end, holds some serious power/technology. He ©©©©ing stops time. Who knows what happens to alyx, I\'m sure she is fine though. Right at the end, he pulls a "matrix" and steps out of the darkness through a door (like the hallways full of doors in the matrix, though cooler and less ©©© than reloaded.) I\'m assuming he has some great technology at his disposle, for whoever his "empolyers" are, and whatever his purpose is and whatever his job actually is.\n\n\n\nYeah, I\'m done.\n\n\n\nEdit: I\'d like to add the part at the Sand Trap level, he says "he was the finest mind of HIS generation" So  I wouldn\'t be surprised to find out that these teleporters do involve time travel and they took brilliant minds from all places and times on earth.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1542957, 178246, 1040, 101656, 1072936800, '', 'Ok, I\'m going to try responding to some of your comments here, since no one else seems to be chiming in. Then I\'ll provide some of my own theories. \n\n\n\nFirst, I really like the idea of the game taking place on another planet. That would be a hell of a twist, but if it were true, I think they would have stuck that in on the end somewhere, just to blow everyone\'s mind at the closing. \n\n\n\nThe comment about the G-Man saying that he likes a survivor, unless I am completely mixed up, comes from Opposing Force. He says it to Shepard as his reasoning for leaving him alive in Xen rather than killing him off somehow. \n\n\n\nDo you know what the quote was about Gordon being given something as a baby? I don\'t think I heard that, but then, I\'ve only played all the way through Half-Life 2 once at this point. What level was it on?\n\n\n\nAs far as Gordon\'s sponsor goes, the G-Man works well for a conspiracy theory idea, but I thought it said somewhere in the original Half-Life manual that Dr. Vance originally sponsored him. I think I recall a VALVe interview somewhere that they said they wanted to bring him in as a character since he only got a mention in the manual and never really in the full game itself. Could be wrong on that, however. \n\n\n\nSomeone offered a theory that Nilanth was also a slave. It was pointed out that he was being harnessed with similar things that the Vortigons were wearing in the original game. I need to recheck this for myself, but if it is true, it could be thought that the Combine were controlling the aliens through Nilanth. We\'ve already seen them do this through utilizing missiles that contain the head-crabs. \n\n\n\nAs for what the Combine are trying to do, first of all, I think the Combine forces you fight throughout the game are not the actual Combine race themselves. There are instances where Dr. Breen says over the City PA system that they are the trans-human Combine army, or something to that effect. Also, there are parts of the game where you can see biological charts of the human head and brain with various points being marked out. I believe the humans that were processed through Nova Prospekt and throughout the Citadel were being surgically altered to become the army of the Combine. What exactly this surgery involved is unknown, but due to the radio readings you would hear after killing the soldiers, we know that their life signs are being monitored at all times. \n\n\n\nAnd the G-Man? The G-Man, especially due to the ending to Half-Life 2, makes me think of this engineering company featured in the book Ice Limit by Douglas Preston and Lincoln Child. Essentially, you paid them, and they would attempt to pull off any task for you, whether it be assassination or, in that book, the removal of a meteorite from a foreign country without alerting any officials. I think the G-Man just works for a company that offers solutions to different groups (whether they be governments or otherwise). If you pay this company enough, they will plant one of their recruits in the situation where it could end up benefitting you. As to how their company has such advanced power and technology? Well, that is definitely up for debate....', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543052, 178246, 1040, 100295, 1072936800, '', 'teh gman is Gordan freeMAN in the future......thats why at the end he was so obsessive about \'time\'....', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543095, 178246, 1040, 101657, 1072936800, '', 'some interesting theores about gman n gordon..\n\n\n\n\n\n"Gordon Freeman is the G-Man"\n\n\n\nI know this is a long shot, but when thought about, doesn\'t seem that crazy or unbelievable... here are some theories that I have to back up this comment:\n\n\n\n1. The names are similar, the name G-Man is composed of Gordon\'s name, i.e Gordon freeMAN = G-Man... just like how, NEO from the matrix was the ONE, an anagram...\n\n\n\n2. In this moment in time, Gordon has the capability to teleport from room to room or from universe to universe, who says that in the future he doesn\'t have the capability to use this teleportation to travel through time? G-Man is obviously older than Gordon, I presume G-Man comes back in time via teleportation to help himself through battle, look over himself through battle to ensure he survives for his future goals etc...\n\n\n\n\n\nwhat do u think?\n\n\n\n3. In the game when Gordon was teleported to the lab,it took a week! He said he will have to do research on that.\n\n\n\n4. The reason why G-Man is able to move from one point in game to another very quickly is because he uses an advanced teleportation device than the one Gordon uses now...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543131, 178246, 1040, 100295, 1072936800, '', 'and the obsessiveness gman has w/ time at the end of the game.. theres no better reason , because thats how they could make room for a hl3 ;o', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543157, 178246, 1040, 101658, 1072936800, '', 'good golly i want hl3 right now!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1543221, 178246, 1040, 101659, 1072936800, '', 'This thread is too good to drown really.\n\n\n\n    I hope the GMan isnt merely a middle-man for some big organisation, I prefer to think of him as some kind of higher-being, and the ending to HL2 would seem to bear that theory out. As would the marvels that Dr Breen was describing to Dr Vance in his office at the citadel.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546092, 178246, 1040, 101656, 1072936800, '', 'I like the G-Man is Gordon theory, but for some reason it just doesn\'t entirely jive with me. Here are my reasons:\n\n\n\n1. First of all, we have never heard anyone in the games refer to the G-Man as the G-Man. That name was just tacked onto the Model if you wanted to use it in Half-Life Multiplayer and it has just stuck ever since. We technically don\'t really know what to call him, unless there is some VALVe interview somewhere I am missing. In my opinion, that makes the G-Man = Gordon freeMAN link really flimsy. \n\n\n\n2. Part of the reason that VALVe never has Gordon speak is because that way, we become Gordon Freeman. He does not know anything that we do not know. Our confusion in the game is the exact same as his. If the G-Man turns out to be Gordon from the future, wouldn\'t that detract from this ideal?\n\n\n\n3. In response to Gordon having the teleportation technology...well....he doesn\'t really. He has been teleported several times, yes, but never through his own devices. Always it was some outside thing that did the teleporting. In the first game, he used pre-established portals and was sent through to Xen by the Science Team. And in Half Life 2, it was Dr. Kleiner\'s teleporter that bounced Gordon around. \n\n\n\n4. The G-Man makes too good of an antangonist. If VALVe made him into a future version of Gordon, there could never be a plot where Gordon tried to escape his forced service. \n\n\n\n5. And besides, if the G-Man was Gordon from the future, the entire series would be a paradox. If you assume that the G-Man is from the future and is coming back in time to set Gordon on the right path, well then who went back in time to set the G-Man on that path back when he was in Gordon\'s shoes? If you assume that the G-Man is changing his own past, couldn\'t he end up erasing his existence of causing some kind of time-space paradox? It just gets too confusing to try to work out, in my opinion. \n\n\n\nAnyone else\'s thoughts?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546200, 178246, 1040, 101615, 1072936800, '', 'Okay, I don\'t have time to respond to all of that, but that part about Ravenholm and the Priest tending his flock---\n\n\n\nGo read the bible if you want to understand that []\n\n\n\nIt has nothing to do with eating, its indicating that these zombies were once humans he knew that were in his congregation.\n\nHe mentions later while killing them that he is letting them rest-freeing their souls.\n\n\n\nThere are many references, in this part and throughout.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546294, 178246, 1040, 100777, 1072936800, '', 'I think that the combine once enslaved the beings of xen, i read in a article in a website (cant remember which one) that the cuffs around the vortigaunts wrists might have been used to control them, and that nihlanth or whatever also had those around his wrists. so i think that the combine are from a diffrent dimention and they want complete control. One thing that supports this theory is that when you are talking to one of the vortigaunts he says "keep fighting, for freedom" or something so i belive that they were once enslaved by the combine as well.\n\n\n\nedit:\n\nOr perhaps the combine are just humans that are being used to control  the human race kinda like they did with the xen, and breen was just a pawn of these yet unseen beings like nihlanth was.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546433, 178246, 1040, 101660, 1072936800, '', 'Just a slight idea on the g-man = freeman thing, \n\n \n\n I was reading everyone else\'s posts, and noticed everyone saying gordon doesnt speak, which is true. BUT, if you listen to G-man talk, he has sort of a speed up, slow down, stutter kind of speech. perhaps that is why gordon never spoke, because he had that problem, seein as how g-man would then = freeman, just a possibility.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546467, 178246, 1040, 101661, 1072936800, '', 'Awesome thread guys...\n\n\n\nI\'m sure (or hope, rather) that Valve will release an expansion that tells more of the story.  \n\n\n\nAs I was reading this, me and my roommate have just been playing HL2 for the past few days nonstop.    He was looking at the thing on Dr. Vance\'s wall that says "Earth surrenders to Combine after 7 hour war" and he says "You should be able to play the 7 hour war"\n\n\n\nAnd then I said\n\n\n\n"As... Adrian Shepard."   He says "Holy ©©©©..."\n\n\n\nValve, we\'re on to something, baby.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546539, 178246, 1040, 101656, 1072936800, '', 'Playing as Shepard during the 7 hour war would be pretty intense. \n\n\n\nThink about it this way: If you do assume that the Combine really were in control of Xen, then in order to partake in the 7 Hour War, Shepard would have to fight his way out of Xen, through the Combine, and back to Earth, ending up right in the middle of this huge war. \n\n\n\nNow that would be cool. \n\n\n\nEDIT: Although on second thought, this does kind of sound a bit like Halo...\n\n\n\nI think Shepard makes the most sense to play as in an expansion back, from a story-telling point of view. With him, they can essentially do anything they wish to, since he has no true ties to the Half-Life 2 plotline yet. With everyone else, that becomes a problem. Playing as Alyx has been mentioned, but you would have to connect that around times when she wasn\'t standing at Gordon\'s side. Playing as Barney would be interesting, but as others have said, he isn\'t incredibly tied into the plot aside from just a grunt\'s point of view. And playing as the G-Man himself...I don\'t know, seeing the G-Man running around with a gun just doesn\'t work for me. It would ruin the all powerful illusion of him we have at this point. \n\n\n\nAnother thing to consider though, is that there are others from Black Mesa we haven\'t heard much about. What happened to Dr. Rosenberg, who we helped escape in Blue Shift? Or, if you want to get really fussy, what about the two female scientists you played as in Co-Op Playstation 2 Half-Life? I haven\'t played it myself, but I assume they escaped as well....', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546605, 178246, 1040, 101662, 1072936800, 'i am stoopid', 'quote:Originally posted by Ash Dizzle \n\nAwesome thread guys...\n\n\n\nI\'m sure (or hope, rather) that Valve will release an expansion that tells more of the story.  \n\n\n\nAs I was reading this, me and my roommate have just been playing HL2 for the past few days nonstop.    He was looking at the thing on Dr. Vance\'s wall that says "Earth surrenders to Combine after 7 hour war" and he says "You should be able to play the 7 hour war"\n\n\n\nAnd then I said\n\n\n\n"As... Adrian Shepard."   He says "Holy ©©©©..."\n\n\n\nValve, we\'re on to something, baby.  \n\n\n\nawww man, I feel like an idiot because I can\'t remember names... but who the hell is Adrian Shepard?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1546662, 178246, 1040, 101656, 1072936800, '', 'He\'s the marine character you play in Opposing Force.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1541924, 178273, 1013, 101663, 1072936800, 'Half-Life 2/Steam', 'Well, I bought Half-Life 2 a few days ago. Day after I bought it, I sent this email to Valve Software. Frankly, I would urge anyone else having "issues" with Half-Life 2 to do the same and really kick it home into the minds of those at Valve that either don\'t know, or do not care about this. Half-Life 2\'s distribution method in no way resembles anything that might be considered acceptable.\n\n\n\nValve Software\'s contact email is: contact@valvesoftware.com (mailto:contact@valvesoftware.com)\n\n--------\n\nHello,\n\n\n\nI\'ve purchased Half-Life 2 today, and have played it some(albet while\n\nbeing required to be online while doing so). Seems to be a fairly\n\ndecent game, though I\'ve not gotten particularly far in it. You guy\'s\n\nhave recaptured the immersive feel of the original Half-Life with the\n\ngameplay. Good job.\n\n\n\nHowever, this isn\'t why I\'m contacting you. Steam is. In a word?\n\nAwful.\n\nI\'ve just now received my "receipt" from Steam regarding my\n\nCD Key. This is six hours after I actually installed the game. (Which\n\ntook two hours itself, as well as several issues with the\n\ninstallation software.)\n\nI\'m not going to rant or tirade like many of those expressing their\n\ndissatisfaction with this arrangement--in a more abrasive manner--on\n\nthe SteamPowered forums.\n\n\n\nI suppose my feelings on how you\'ve handled Half-Life 2 are best\n\nsummed up like so:\n\nIf you wanted your customers and fans never to come back, all you had\n\nto do was ask.\n\n\n\nMuch easier than spending a few years developing Steam and HL2, I\n\nwould think.\n\nI wish you well on your future business, but will never again\n\npurchase any software you\'ve bundled with Steam. PC Games should not\n\nbe more complex or painstaking to install and configure than\n\noperating systems.\n\n\n\nRegards,\n\nJason Hutton\n\n--------\n\nAlso posted here:\n\nhttp://www.livejournal.com/users/xbahamut/ (http://www.livejournal.com/users/xbahamut/)', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1541979, 178273, 1013, 101664, 1072936800, '', 'ive used both steam gold pack and retail version both installed and played with no problems what so ever.\n\nwhat took 2 hours to install it took me maybe 10 minutes or so', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1542252, 178273, 1013, 101665, 1072936800, '', 'Excellent letter.  \n\n\n\nFOr my own part, I ranted and screamed and swore at them 8 times or so that day as well as leaving two voice mails for Gabe.\n\n\n\nIt didn\'t help with the game or steam or anything, but I felt a whole lot better.\n\n\n\nI was patient up until Tuesday, at that point, I felt I had been raped or otherwise violated after believing them for over a year.\n\n\n\nI\'m probably naive but I actually believed the 9/30/2003 date and even bought the game on 9/1/2003.\n\n\n\nI don\'t even know what steam is yet.  All the cultists sound like its the same as the sun rising in the east, but I paid for H-L2, not steam and I don\'t really care what it is.\n\n\n\nWith Zone Alarm, I found I can play w/o letting steam report to these evil barnyards, so that\'s kind of good.\n\n\n\nAnd I\'ve been taking my residual anger out in the forums and on the kool-aid drinkers who insist steam is perfect.\n\n\n\nLOL!  It cracks me up that people can be so deluded.  But then, I believed in 9/30/2003 so I guess I shouldn\'t really laugh at the poor brain dead zombies as I was one once myself.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1542667, 178273, 1013, 101666, 1072936800, '', 'Nobody has ever said Steam is perfect to my recollection. I just feel people are overreacting. I\'ve never had any real issues with Steam that didn\'t resolve on their own with patience. This clues me in that all those that are complaining are mostly impatient.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549110, 179201, 1040, 101667, 1072936800, 'Retail users CD / DVD check solution', 'Sticky would be nice!\n\n\n\nI posted this in another thread. \n\n\n\nI did this for my retail install of Condition Zero (which also required the CD to run) and when I have some spare time I\'ll do it for HL2 and CS:Source.\n\n\n\nOnce you have registered your CD-KEY with Valve through steam, If you can be bothered to download all the HL2 and CS:source content you can play without the DVD. Just create a new steam install in a different location, open that install of steam and download the content through steam. HL2 and CS:source will appear is the "My Games" list and you can install them from there. Same way ATI vouchers had a CD Key your CD Key is registered to your Steam account and it\'s that key that allows you to download the content that you have purchased.  This should install it the same way as everyone who purchased it through steam meaning you won\'t have to use the DVD to play it.\n\n\n\nVivendi are responsable for making sure the DVD\'s etc are not copied and distributed easily, you can\'t blame them for that considering the Piracy these days.\n\n\n\nIt\'s a lot of pissing about but it works. I\'d like to see Valve fix this for us retail users but it\'s unlikely as it will probably breach their contract with Vivendi or something and cause more problems. \n\n\n\nThis may be our only solution\n\n\n\nSTICKY STICKY STICKY\n\n\n\nthere are a few threads about this subject already!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549147, 179201, 1040, 101668, 1072936800, '', 'So basicly you just re-download all of the half-life 2 content?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549217, 179201, 1040, 101667, 1072936800, '', 'not exactly.\n\n\n\nYou need to install a seperate version of steam, either on another partition or just a different folder...eg. c:\\MySteam\\Steam\n\n\n\nAnything as long as it\'s not your default Steam install. Right click and exit your existing steam install before you do this though. Then log into the new steam install with your existing account details by double clicking the steam.exe file and then download the content through steam. This version of steam will not detect your games installed on your previous install of steam BUT once logged in it will recognise your account and allow you to install HL2 and CS:Source.\n\n\n\nPlease rate this thread if you think it\'s helpful\n\n\n\nI know it\'s harsh having to download all the content even though you\'ve got the disk but it\'s the only way I know of.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549219, 179201, 1040, 101669, 1072936800, '', 'yeah.. lets spend an extra $40 or so on a dvd that you hardly even use...  just get the silver pack through steam...  its cheaper and u get more than the "collectors" edition.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549233, 179201, 1040, 101667, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by unleashed \n\nyeah.. lets spend an extra $40 or so on a dvd that you hardly even use...  just get the silver pack through steam...  its cheaper and u get more than the "collectors" edition.  \n\n\n\nthat\'s not really what this thread is about but I only paid �26.99 for my DVD. I\'m just trying to help people like me who are pissed off with having to disk swap whenever they play CS:Source or HL2', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549247, 179201, 1040, 101670, 1072936800, '', 'This deserves a sticky, or at least a bump!\n\n\n\nBUMP!\n\n\n\nLet\'s help our technology scared friends!', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549291, 179201, 1040, 101671, 1072936800, '', 'So you are saying I got an DVD just to ignore it and download the game instead, which was the very reason I bought the DVD (lowly ADSL would take a week to download HL2)?\n\n\n\nBrilliant solution...\n\n\n\nI think Valve need to add the option to:\n\n\n\nSTEAM ONLINE - NO CD REQUIRED\n\nSTEAM OFFLINE - CD REQUIRED \n\n\n\nSimple as that. No warez issues, since I assume steam will catch on if you use an illegal account.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549304, 179201, 1040, 101672, 1072936800, '', 'quote:No warez issues, since I assume steam will catch on if you use an illegal account.\n\n\n\nThe problem is that in theory you could buy the DVD, register the game, return the DVD, then not only do you have a \'free\' copy, but whoever buys the DVD after you will find their CD key is already registered...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549339, 179201, 1040, 101673, 1072936800, '', 'redownloading now. thx for info.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549410, 179201, 1040, 101667, 1072936800, '', 'thanks mate!\n\n\n\nokay laymans terms, do this:\n\n\n\n1. Exit Steam\n\n\n\n2. Install a new installation of Steam (just steam nothing else) to a different folder or partition. Eg. C:\\My Steam\\Steam or d:\\Program Files\\Steam if you have a D drive with plenty of space. Whatever as long as it\'s not the same place as your existing Steam install.\n\n\n\n3. Log into the new Steam install with your existing steam account.\n\n\n\n4. Goto play games and in the "My games" section you\'ll see that HL2 and CS:Source are not highlighted or \'lit up\'.\n\n\n\n5. Steam recognises what games you are entitled to by the account you have with Valve, therefore it knows what your allowed to download. It\'s no different to those that had ATI voucher CD keys, were entitled to the software in the same way they are.\n\n\n\n6. Double click it/ right click choose properties - whatever it will then let you install them through steam which will download all the files you need. This way you won\'t have to portion of the retail install that tells the game to check you DVD is inserted.\n\n\n\n7. Play the game and never complain again!\n\n\n\nHope this helps people who like me are fed up with swapping disks, I don\'t like no-cd cracks. Apart from being illegal you never know what malicious code is in them.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549412, 179201, 1040, 101674, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by MarkG \n\nThe problem is that in theory you could buy the DVD, register the game, return the DVD, then not only do you have a \'free\' copy, but whoever buys the DVD after you will find their CD key is already registered...  \n\n\n\nSo? How many people would do that? Do shops even accept an opened game for return? It is really such an issue that they needed to piss off all their retail customers? Besides, you don\'t need a CD for the original Half-Life, meaning you could do the same with it, but that didn\'t bother Valve?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549460, 179201, 1040, 101667, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by merka \n\nSo you are saying I got an DVD just to ignore it and download the game instead, which was the very reason I bought the DVD (lowly ADSL would take a week to download HL2)?\n\n\n\nBrilliant solution...\n\n\n\nI think Valve need to add the option to:\n\n\n\nSTEAM ONLINE - NO CD REQUIRED\n\nSTEAM OFFLINE - CD REQUIRED \n\n\n\nSimple as that. No warez issues, since I assume steam will catch on if you use an illegal account.  \n\n\n\nI agree with you 100% but at the moment this is the only way and I doubt that will change because Vivendi will probably be a bit annoyed with Valve if they just disable their anti piracy measures. \n\n\n\nI\'m just trying to help the people that really want a solution.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549470, 179201, 1040, 101675, 1072936800, 'Does this actually work?', 'Has anyone got this to work? \n\n\n\nI hope it works (I\'m in the same boat) but I\'m a bit dubious, for this reason: if you have the CD-key version on your Steam account, you may get the retail (.i.e. containing "source shared securom.gcf") version when you download it from Steam, since Steam knows you\'ve purchased the retail version, as opposed the online only product-key version.  So you\'ll still need your original CD/DVD to run the game once you\'ve downloaded it. \n\n\n\n\n\nI\'m trying this suggestion now. Only 200 minutes until I find out if it works.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549481, 179201, 1040, 101674, 1072936800, '', 'Sorry, but IT WILL NOT WORK. Steam knows by your CD-Key that you have the retail version and will re-download the "source shared securom.gcf" file with the protection too and use it when starting the game...\n\n\n\nEDIT: ahhh, amphion beat me to it by a minute...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1549483, 179201, 1040, 101672, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Besides, you don\'t need a CD for the original Half-Life, meaning you could do the same with it, but that didn\'t bother Valve?\n\n\n\nBut you only have half the problem with an offline game: people can pirate it by installing it and returning the CD, but you don\'t get irate customers finding they can\'t play a game they just bought because the CD key has been stolen and registered.\n\n\n\nAlso, I suspect that it\'s more an issue for publishers than for Valve. Valve would probably prefer that everyone bought from Steam in the first place [].', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1555897, 179984, 1040, 100307, 1072936800, '', 'Steam Client Update Released\n\nNovember 19, 2004\n\n\n\nA small number of Half-Life 2 customers have been experiencing problems while playing the game, and we have traced these issues to corrupt Steam cache files. We\'ve released a Steam update (/index.php?area=news&id=351) to help resolve these issues. If you have been experiencing problems such as crashing during gameplay or startup of Half-Life 2, please restart Steam and then click on one of the links below. Steam will then automatically verify your cache files. The verification process will take roughly five minutes to complete.\n\n\n\nTo validate Half-Life 2 click here (steam://validate/220)\n\n\n\nTo Validate Counter-Strike: Source click here (steam://validate/240)\n\n\n\nTo Validate Half-Life 1: Source click here (steam://validate/280)\n\n\n\nWe\'re also investigating a solution for the sound skipping problems a small number of users are running into. We\'ll have a solution for this as soon as possible.\n\n\n\n\n\nreturn to news page', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1555915, 179984, 1040, 101676, 1072936800, '', 'Man lucky me...  I wish there was an option to do it.  Mine did it automatically although everything is working fine and I\'ve been waiting probably 30 minutes for it to validate.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1555950, 179984, 1040, 101677, 1072936800, '', 'small number? look at the size of that Audio Stuttering thread! hehe', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1555956, 179984, 1040, 101678, 1072936800, '', 'Shouldnt steam have the option to validate the files anyway ?\n\nI think those links should be built in to the properties tab for each game, so if you want to check them in the future you can.\n\n\n\nPlease suggest it to the dev team', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1555967, 179984, 1040, 101679, 1072936800, '', '"a small number"?? It\'s small percentage of the owners perhaps, but there is certainly a large amount of people that are experiencing this problem...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1555997, 179984, 1040, 101680, 1072936800, '', 'bump once again!!\n\n\n\nVALVE RESPOND!!!!!\n\n\n\nwe paid for your product , unlike pirates out there,\n\n\n\nso show us the same respect back and give us a fix or even acknowlage that a fix is being worked on.\n\n\n\nif there was ever a reson to turn people to the dark side to buy pirate copys id say this is the time!\n\n\n\nyou dont deserve the loyalty we have shown\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWE WANT NO CD/DVD PATCH FOR ONLINE USERS', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556014, 179984, 1040, 101681, 1072936800, '', 'hahaha nice joke: small number of users.... i know 3 guys they dont have any \n\nstutter etc. and theyre blind and deaf and dont play games.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556020, 179984, 1040, 101682, 1072936800, '', 'Small number?  That\'s the understatement of the year.  150,000+ views and 1000+ post on this forum alone.  Small...', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556048, 179984, 1040, 101683, 1072936800, '', 'What happens when the validation completes?  Is it supposed to find errors and download something else, or is the validation process all that is needed for this to (possibly) fix the crashes we\'re having?\n\n\n\nThanks!\n\n\n\n--Rory', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556073, 179984, 1040, 101610, 1072936800, '', 'Well at least be happy they investigated it :P I\'m now verifying HL2 files. Plz let that g*dd*mn "Could not validate the game disc within the time limit" error be fixed ! :P I bought the freakin\' retail []', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556115, 179984, 1040, 101684, 1072936800, '', 'I assume you need to dl this update if you are only having the crash problem?\n\n\n\nI have the sound studdering, which is quite annoying, so I am happy that they are looking into that.\n\n\n\nIt also takes awhile for loading to occur, but I helped that out a bit my upgrading my RAM from 512 to 1GB.\n\n\n\nI guess I will skip this update, unless they say it\'s necessary for everyone.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556116, 179984, 1040, 101685, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by insanewords \n\nSmall number?  That\'s the understatement of the year.  150,000+ views and 1000+ post on this forum alone.  Small...  \n\n\n\nLol, is there actually anyone without that problem?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556118, 179984, 1040, 100307, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by HL2Pwnage \n\nWell at least be happy they investigated it :P[...] \n\n\n\nThank you...  Someone who thinks outside the box.', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556130, 179984, 1040, 101686, 1072936800, '', 'quote:Originally posted by insanewords \n\nSmall number?  That\'s the understatement of the year.  150,000+ views and 1000+ post on this forum alone.  Small...  small my a**.  I wonder what they consider big', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1556135, 179984, 1040, 101687, 1072936800, 'Steam Client Update Released', 'quote:Originally posted by smash \n\nA small number of Half-Life 2 customers have been experiencing problems while playing the game, and we have traced these issues to corrupt Steam cache files. We\'ve released a Steam update (/index.php?area=news&id=351) to help resolve these issues. If you have been experiencing problems such as crashing during gameplay or startup of Half-Life 2, please restart Steam and then click on one of the links below. Steam will then automatically verify your cache files. The verification process will take roughly five minutes to complete.\n\n\n\nTo validate Half-Life 2 click here (steam://validate/220)\n\n\n\nTo Validate Counter-Strike: Source click (steam://validate/240) here\n\n\n\nTo Validate Half-Life 1: Source click here (steam://validate/280)\n\n\n\nWe\'re also investigating a solution for the sound skipping problems a small number of users are running into. We\'ll have a solution for this as soon as possible.\n\n\n\n\n\nreturn to news page  \n\n\n\na small number?? thats a joke right?', '127.0.0.1', 1),
(1563236, 180793, 1040, 101688, 1072936800, 'G-Man is dead! (Bug)', 'I finished the game ealier today.  Tonight I felt like driving the scout car and smashing some antlions, so I loaded up the coast level and started driving and smashing away.\n\n\n\nI didn\'t stop at any of the houses along the way, except the one where I ran over the soldiers.  I drove all the way to the town where you meet with that anoying British guy and fight the gunship.  I stopped just before I got there, but that guy still came running up to me and told me to get to the basement.  I ignored him, turned my car around and drove back the other way smashing more antlions.\n\n\n\nI got to the house where the soldiers are with the binocular things.  I killed the soldiers and looked to see if the g-man was still there.  He was, but after it made it\'s first pass he didn\'t dissapear like he did before.\n\n\n\nI get back in the car, drive back to the town and sure enough, he\'s still standing there with the British guy.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...75/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559275/original.jpg)\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...76/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559276/original.jpg)\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...77/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559277/original.jpg)\n\n\n\nI go upstairs for a closer look, and for some reason the Brisish guy runs from me.  He ran around the side, but the G-Man stayed there.  He\'d even look at me every few seconds.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...78/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559278/original.jpg)\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...79/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559279/original.jpg)\n\n\n\nI ran downstairs to see if the Brittish guy would be there, and he was.  Then I ran back up to see if he was still up top, he\'d dissapeared from up there, but G-Man was still there.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...80/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559280/original.jpg)\n\n\n\nI couldn\'t shoot him, so I went back to the basement to grabbed the rocket launcher so the attack would begin.  I ran back upstairs just in time to see the alien gunship mow down G-Man. []\n\n\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...81/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559281/original.jpg)\n\n\n\nAfter I killed the gunship, the G-Man was still there, his bony hands still clutching that brief case.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...82/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559282/original.jpg)\n\n\n\nNow I could shoot him and watch his body jump around, it felt good for some reason. []  No matter how many rounds I put into his dead body, he wouldn\'t let go of that damned briefcase.  I guess we\'ll never find out what\'s inside.\n\n\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...83/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559283/original.jpg)\n\nhttp://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/...68/original.jpg (http://www.pbase.com/jchaney/image/36559568/original.jpg)', '127.0.0.1', 1),
